WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:10.830
- Hello, everyone.

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:14.200
This is the 2021 Finzi-Contini Lecture

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:15.983
at the Whitney Humanities Center.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.710
I'm Paul North, the interim director.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:21.160
Alice Kaplan, the current director,

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:22.510
will be back in the spring.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:27.560
I'm thinking of a contrast

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:31.330
in the movie by Vittorio De Sica from 1971

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:33.507
called "The Garden of the Finzi-Continis."

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:37.360
De Sica shows the huge
generosity of spirit

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.710
of this Italian-Jewish family.

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:41.010
And then the shock and dismay

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:44.573
when they're brutally displaced
by fascists and antisemites.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.030
A tennis court in Milan
full of young people

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:49.620
at the beginning of the film;

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:52.670
the painfully empty
tennis court at the end.

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:55.450
Both of those characteristics
are present each year

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:58.410
in this lecture named after the family,

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:01.180
when the family's continuing
generosity makes it possible

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:02.900
to hear a literary writer,

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:04.550
who was often writing from the thick

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:06.093
of current global crisis.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:11.970
The Finzi-Contini lectureship
was endowed in 1990

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:14.300
by the Honorable Guido Calabresi,

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:17.380
Judge, US Court of Appeals
for the Second Circuit

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:19.490
and former Dean of the Yale Law School,

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:22.971
and by his brother, the
late Dr. Paul Calabresi.

00:01:22.971 --> 00:01:25.330
The lecture honors their
mother, grandmother

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:30.330
and great-grandmother, speaking
of the family as a whole,

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:34.300
Bianca Maria Finzi-Contini Calabresi.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:37.110
She and her husband, Massimo Calabresi,

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:40.330
activists in the resistance
against Italian fascism,

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:42.590
fled Italy in 1939

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:46.170
after the passage of the
first infamous racial laws.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.800
They settled in New Haven to start anew.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:51.653
He as a physician, she
has a literary scholar.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.970
Bianca Maria Finzi-Contini Calabresi

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:59.430
earned her PhD in French at Yale in 1949,

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.323
with a dissertation on Ernest Renan.

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:04.500
Her subject was one of the
most fascinating writers

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.320
of the 19th century.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:09.370
A liberal and a brilliant
analyst of nationalism

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:12.660
and Christianity, who believed
in racial hierarchies,

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:14.970
but nonetheless counted among his enemies

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:17.333
the most vicious antisemites in France.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.410
Her thesis was directed by Henri Peyre,

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:23.030
a European exile from Paris

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:25.830
who wanted to see her appointed
to the faculty at Yale,

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:29.330
but the 1949 Yale Corporation refused.

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:32.150
There would be no tenured
women on the faculty at Yale

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:33.563
for another three years.

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:38.470
Bianca Maria Finzi-Contini
Calabresi was undeterred.

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:41.330
She went on to become
professor of French and Italian

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:42.870
at Connecticut College,

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:44.840
and then chair of the
Department of Italian

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:47.220
at Albertus Magnus College.

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:49.970
Her last published volume,
also written in French,

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:52.760
was on Renan's friendship
with Emile Egger,

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:55.960
one of the founders of classic philology.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:58.443
She died in 1982, at the age of 80.

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:02.570
We wish to thank Judge
Calabresi and his family

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:05.240
for their generosity, and
for giving us the privilege

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:07.640
of remembering our distinguished alumna,

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:10.730
Bianca Maria Finzi-Contini Calabresi.

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:13.160
Greetings to the three
generations of Calabresis,

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:15.063
who are in the audience this evening.

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:17.910
The Finzi-Contini Lectures

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:19.620
sponsors a distinguished speaker

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:23.370
in the field of comparative
literature, broadly defined.

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:26.270
In 1991, the inaugural lecture was given

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:29.980
by the great Italian
semiotician Umberto Eco.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:32.070
Subsequent lectures in
this series have been given

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:35.330
by a now long list of eminent
writers from around the world,

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:38.100
including Tzvetan Todorov, A.S. Byatt,

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:41.980
Orhan Pamuk, Masha Gessen, Azar Nafisi,

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:44.563
and Maxine Hong Kingston
among many others.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:48.750
Beginning with our March
2021 Finzi-Contini Lecture,

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:52.040
The Yale Review has joined
the Whitney as co-sponsor.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:54.450
Please look for today's talk in print,

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:56.063
in its pages and online.

00:03:57.140 --> 00:04:00.130
Race Off will appear
in the fall 2021 issue

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:03.270
of The Yale Review
edited by Megan O'Rourke,

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:05.013
who we thank for all her help.

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:10.479
Now, let me welcome Namwali Serpell.

00:04:10.479 --> 00:04:13.180
Professor of English at Harvard,

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:14.290
previously a professor

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:16.600
at the University of California, Berkeley,

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.330
literary critic, theorist of colonialism

00:04:19.330 --> 00:04:22.470
and global struggles around
race and post-coloniality,

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:26.190
and just as importantly,
a fiction writer of note.

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:29.610
Her first published story "Muzungu"

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:33.130
was selected for The Best
American Short Stories 2009

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:35.770
and short listed for the 2010 Caine Prize

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:37.620
for African writing.

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:41.647
She also won the 2015 Caine
Prize for her story "The Sack."

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:45.490
Her first book of literary criticism

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:47.610
called "Seven Modes of Uncertainty"

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:51.090
was published by Harvard
University Press in 2014.

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:53.320
And our first novel, "The Old Drift"

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:56.280
came out with Hogarth Press in 2019,

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:58.220
and won accolades as diverse

00:04:58.220 --> 00:05:00.970
as the Anisfield-Wolf
Book award for fiction

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:04.510
and the Arthur C. Clarke
Award for science fiction,

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:05.700
as well as being included

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:08.570
among the hundred notable books of 2019

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:10.160
by the New York Times

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:13.193
and Time Magazine's 100
Must-Read Books of the Year.

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:17.010
Must-read books.

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:19.960
About those words, must and read,

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:22.633
they really don't go
together, or shouldn't.

00:05:23.710 --> 00:05:25.960
Aesthetic experience
is supposed to be free

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:29.810
in a way that the moral
must doesn't allow.

00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:31.900
We can read about all sorts
of things that we should

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:34.113
by no means than go and do.

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:38.110
For Serpell, literature
is free in this way.

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:41.100
It, quote, enlivens and, quote, activates.

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:43.677
I'm quoting from "Seven
Modes of Uncertainty".

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:47.023
But there's a special
obligation present too.

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:49.820
There's a longer quote from that book.

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:52.550
Quote, literature produces free choice.

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:54.940
We have to decide what something means.

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:57.543
But this free choice is
forced on us by the text.

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:00.730
This is a line from the
scholarly and critical book

00:06:00.730 --> 00:06:03.530
from 2014 "Seven Modes of Uncertainty,"

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:06.670
and you can see how she's
changing up the obligation.

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:09.840
As a reader, you choose
what something means freely,

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:12.250
but you have no choice but to choose.

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:13.383
So how do you do it?

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:17.123
The first step is to
recognize uncertainty.

00:06:18.170 --> 00:06:20.970
Uncertainty is not primarily
an epistemic problem,

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:22.740
a problem with knowledge.

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:25.700
I don't know, or I don't
know whether I know or not,

00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:26.703
which is bad enough.

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:29.330
But it's a problem that lies in, quote,

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:31.920
the recesses of the word ethics.

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:34.770
Maybe I should act, and maybe I shouldn't.

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:36.320
Maybe what I'm doing or plan to do

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:37.870
won't even count as action

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:40.933
in this singular and complex scenario.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:44.360
When encountering a complex
scene in literature,

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:46.320
this is one step more difficult,

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:48.550
do I read a scene this way or that,

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:51.410
or can it be read in both ways at once?

00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:54.370
This is the highest point of
tension for literary critics,

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:56.350
where they usually give up and say,

00:06:56.350 --> 00:06:58.820
many interpretations are possible.

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:01.930
Serpell tarries with the tension.

00:07:01.930 --> 00:07:04.380
We can't give up either interpretation.

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:07.170
Situations are by nature compound,

00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:10.180
coming from multiple
perspectives and multiple paths

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:12.570
and heading toward multiple futures.

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:13.573
What is to be done?

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:16.030
This is where a reader of literature

00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:18.390
confronts the conundrum
of ethical judgment

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:20.323
in a direct and unabashed way.

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:22.720
And this is a good thought

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:25.410
with which to approach "The Old Drift",

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:27.560
Serpell's prize-winning novel.

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:29.530
I quote from the Windham Campbell website,

00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:32.960
which awarded her its
prestigious prize in 2020,

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:37.230
quote, "The Old Drift" tells
the story of three families,

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:40.740
with people of African,
European, and Indian descent,

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:44.440
living in Zambia over
the course of 200 years.

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:48.590
Part historical adventure,
part psychological realism,

00:07:48.590 --> 00:07:52.010
part futuristic thriller,
and part magical realism,

00:07:52.010 --> 00:07:55.270
the novel is an audacious,
lush, sprawling,

00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:57.130
and altogether brilliant celebration

00:07:57.130 --> 00:07:58.483
of the artifice of fiction.

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:02.493
It's also a laboratory
for readerly ethics.

00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:06.480
For the beginning of the
novel, Percy M Clark,

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:09.200
a white European,
arrives at The Old Drift,

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:10.750
that part of the Zambezi River

00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:13.670
that's not too dangerous to be crossed.

00:08:13.670 --> 00:08:18.670
Clark is a pathetic, minor, sad
version of accessible roads,

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:20.640
an imperialist of the small

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:22.990
whose casual and unquestioned racism

00:08:22.990 --> 00:08:26.970
and thoughtless day-to-day
brutality you can never accept,

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:29.210
and yet the routine hardships of his life,

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:32.080
losing a baby, struggling to earn enough,

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:33.580
fitting his meager intelligence

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:35.550
into the colonial hierarchies,

00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:38.900
make the ethical judgment
harder and more pressing.

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:42.300
What would judging and
sentencing this colonist do?

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:43.500
What would it have done?

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:46.470
Devastation and domination happened

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:49.030
through a million almost
normal lives like this one,

00:08:49.030 --> 00:08:52.590
or rather the almost normal,
as I came to feel electrically

00:08:52.590 --> 00:08:56.740
in my bones when reading the
book, is riddled horrors.

00:08:56.740 --> 00:09:00.000
Clark shoots a black boy
in the back with buckshot,

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:02.153
mistaking him for an animal in the brush.

00:09:03.340 --> 00:09:05.720
I want to single out an
important insight here

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:08.240
that once the imperial project is set up,

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:11.180
horrors come to look
like collateral damage.

00:09:11.180 --> 00:09:12.860
And what to do with an indirect evil

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:15.430
that is almost coincident with normal life

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:20.320
was very vocabulary collateral
damage, conceals it again.

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:23.580
This is the main task of
an ethics of uncertainty

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:24.873
and ethics of reading.

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:28.360
One answer is to allow our response

00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:30.120
to let the other side speak,

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:34.730
which Serpell does in
addenda to each chapter,

00:09:34.730 --> 00:09:38.890
where a pre-national,
even pre-human collective

00:09:38.890 --> 00:09:41.543
judges from a cold,
unsympathetic distance.

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:46.240
The book is responded to
by a chorus of mosquitoes,

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:48.790
who tell us about the unconquerable parts

00:09:48.790 --> 00:09:50.900
of the conquered space.

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:53.360
Parts that are too big, too hot, too wet,

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:56.590
that change too much,
full of uncertainties.

00:09:56.590 --> 00:09:59.437
Quote, this is what the mosquitoes say.

00:09:59.437 --> 00:10:03.417
"Evolution forge the entirety
of life using only one tool:

00:10:03.417 --> 00:10:05.023
"the mistake," unquote.

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:07.693
As you can see,

00:10:07.693 --> 00:10:11.420
Professor Serpell doesn't
run away from complexity,

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:13.630
and her project of a non-simplifying

00:10:13.630 --> 00:10:16.280
ethical education continues.

00:10:16.280 --> 00:10:19.280
She wants the fullest possible
description of a scene

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:21.340
and recognition of its singular conditions

00:10:21.340 --> 00:10:22.830
and multiple perspectives

00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:25.483
before anyone is allowed to say must.

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:28.900
I believe we're being invited
to follow her tonight,

00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:31.110
doing something similar in her lecture,

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:34.600
Race Off: The Fantasy
of Race Transformation.

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:36.450
After the lecture,
there'll be a conversation

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:39.430
with Tina Post, who is
assistant professor of English

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:42.440
and theater and performance
at the University of Chicago

00:10:42.440 --> 00:10:44.410
and a recent alumni of Yale,

00:10:44.410 --> 00:10:46.800
as Professor Serpell herself is,

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:48.573
though, in the more distant past.

00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:52.450
Before I present Professor Serpell,

00:10:52.450 --> 00:10:55.720
I want to mention a couple of issues.

00:10:55.720 --> 00:10:58.550
When you want to ask a question,
please feel free to do it.

00:10:58.550 --> 00:11:02.140
You can put your questions
in the Q&amp;A at any time.

00:11:02.140 --> 00:11:05.520
Chat will be disabled,
as well hand-raising,

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:08.180
but you're free, as I
say, to add your questions

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:09.520
all the way through.

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:12.380
When I read the questions
at the end of the Q&amp;A,

00:11:12.380 --> 00:11:17.380
I will be alighting people's
names for rights reasons.

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:20.810
So now, without any further talk from me,

00:11:20.810 --> 00:11:23.663
please join me in welcoming
Professor Namwali Serpell.

00:11:27.550 --> 00:11:28.930
- Hello, everyone.

00:11:28.930 --> 00:11:30.840
Thank you so much for that wonderful

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:34.300
and complex introduction, Paul.

00:11:34.300 --> 00:11:35.950
I'm going to share my screen

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:39.530
and launch right into my presentation,

00:11:39.530 --> 00:11:41.690
which is called Race Off:

00:11:41.690 --> 00:11:43.480
The Fantasy of Race Transformation,

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:45.133
American Race Transformation.

00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:49.223
And it should be about 50 minutes.

00:11:53.560 --> 00:11:55.663
What if you could change your race?

00:11:56.820 --> 00:11:58.730
Some disturbing scandals of late

00:11:58.730 --> 00:12:02.500
have put this hypothetical
to the reality test.

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:06.460
A cluster of white American
academics and activists,

00:12:06.460 --> 00:12:08.670
many of them women it seems,

00:12:08.670 --> 00:12:10.640
have been revealed to have spent years

00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:13.300
cosplaying a different race.

00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:16.760
Latinx, black, Native American,

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:19.360
misleading, if not outright deceiving

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:20.860
their colleagues and comrades.

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:23.873
Such deceptions are nothing new.

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:26.140
In the mid-19th century,

00:12:26.140 --> 00:12:29.840
P.T. Barnum showcased
people have concocted races

00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:33.483
such as the Circassian girl
or the Circassian beauty.

00:12:34.330 --> 00:12:37.970
Barnum also promoted a
negro, William Cannell,

00:12:37.970 --> 00:12:40.300
who claimed to have discovered a weed

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:42.553
that turns a black person white.

00:12:43.810 --> 00:12:48.810
Newspaper at the time called
out runaway slave impostors.

00:12:49.250 --> 00:12:52.090
At the same moment, white
writers were publishing

00:12:52.090 --> 00:12:54.325
fake slave narratives.

00:12:54.325 --> 00:12:58.697
And as 2017 history bunk,
Kevin Young notes that

00:12:58.697 --> 00:13:02.957
"the hoax regularly steps
in when race rears its head,

00:13:02.957 --> 00:13:06.027
"exactly because it too is a fake thing

00:13:06.027 --> 00:13:07.747
"pretending to be real."

00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:12.420
Indeed, historical evidence
and scientific consensus

00:13:12.420 --> 00:13:15.300
both tell us that race is a social,

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:18.260
rather than a biological reality.

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:21.420
Yet we harbor a near-atavistic obsession

00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:24.273
with the methods and
motives of race fakers.

00:13:25.290 --> 00:13:30.290
How do they change their
skin, their hair, their voice,

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:34.260
their origin story, and why?

00:13:34.260 --> 00:13:35.970
Is it minstrelsy?

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:38.000
Is it white guilt?

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:39.003
Is it fetishism?

00:13:39.990 --> 00:13:42.540
Some have argued that being not white

00:13:42.540 --> 00:13:45.470
has come to seem politically profitable.

00:13:45.470 --> 00:13:49.513
Suffering is now established
as a form of cultural capital.

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:52.890
Today, I want to suggest to you

00:13:52.890 --> 00:13:54.930
that these recent race fakers

00:13:54.930 --> 00:13:57.950
are merely the latest
players in a game I call

00:13:57.950 --> 00:14:00.893
the fantasy of American
race transformation.

00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:04.720
If we take a lead from P.T. Barnum,

00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:08.060
we can taxonomize this
genre along various axes:

00:14:08.060 --> 00:14:11.030
when these texts appeared
and who produced them,

00:14:11.030 --> 00:14:14.620
which race gets transformed
and into what race,

00:14:14.620 --> 00:14:17.830
what mechanism catalyzes it and how,

00:14:17.830 --> 00:14:21.780
is it imposed or
self-motivated and to what end,

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:24.860
is it reversible or permanent?

00:14:24.860 --> 00:14:26.460
While there's a veritable circus

00:14:26.460 --> 00:14:28.930
of racial chimeras to consider,

00:14:28.930 --> 00:14:30.680
I'll train my spotlight today

00:14:30.680 --> 00:14:33.950
on the transformations
between black and white people

00:14:33.950 --> 00:14:35.570
and popular narrative works

00:14:35.570 --> 00:14:40.570
created by black and white
Americans from the 1850s to now.

00:14:40.930 --> 00:14:44.520
From passing to cross-racial reportage

00:14:44.520 --> 00:14:46.640
to speculative fiction,

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:50.430
the fantasy of race transformation
cuts an electric line,

00:14:50.430 --> 00:14:53.920
thrilling, dangerous, illuminating,

00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:56.073
through the American popular imagination.

00:14:57.240 --> 00:15:00.070
At first glance, there might
seem to be something radical

00:15:00.070 --> 00:15:02.450
in the very idea of changing your race,

00:15:02.450 --> 00:15:05.890
which assumes that race is
changeable and therefore unfixed,

00:15:05.890 --> 00:15:07.343
in short, a construct.

00:15:08.440 --> 00:15:10.960
But what we find is that
all the aesthetic energy

00:15:10.960 --> 00:15:13.110
and race transformation stories

00:15:13.110 --> 00:15:16.170
springs from the surprise
of a black person

00:15:16.170 --> 00:15:19.220
inside a white person or vice versa,

00:15:19.220 --> 00:15:23.110
and this, of course, assumes
an essential racial soul

00:15:23.110 --> 00:15:26.100
lurking within the transformed body.

00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:28.650
With this in mind, we see
that to change your race

00:15:28.650 --> 00:15:31.120
is really just a kind
of thought experiment

00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:33.560
geared to make you
empathize with another race,

00:15:33.560 --> 00:15:35.790
to punish you for being bigoted,

00:15:35.790 --> 00:15:40.240
or to grant you brief giddy
access to being another person,

00:15:40.240 --> 00:15:43.190
a kind of racial exoticism or slumming

00:15:43.190 --> 00:15:45.260
that lets you transcend

00:15:45.260 --> 00:15:48.210
the suffocation of social
divisions immediately,

00:15:48.210 --> 00:15:50.340
yet impermanently.

00:15:50.340 --> 00:15:51.900
Race transformation tales

00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:55.260
offer the promise of escaping the body,

00:15:55.260 --> 00:15:59.840
but they often end up making
that body abject or an object.

00:15:59.840 --> 00:16:03.900
In this sense, this
genre reinscribes racism

00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:05.523
rather than rising above it.

00:16:06.460 --> 00:16:08.790
But it does give us some insight,

00:16:08.790 --> 00:16:11.640
not into what race actually is,

00:16:11.640 --> 00:16:13.810
but into what we think it is.

00:16:13.810 --> 00:16:16.070
And naturally, what we think it is

00:16:16.070 --> 00:16:19.463
very much depends on who we mean by we.

00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:26.760
Let me introduce you to this
race transformation circus.

00:16:26.760 --> 00:16:30.210
The grounds are passing narratives

00:16:30.210 --> 00:16:33.590
in which a person deemed
by law to be black

00:16:33.590 --> 00:16:36.083
has features that pass for white.

00:16:36.920 --> 00:16:40.450
The passing narrative first
appeared in 19th century drama

00:16:40.450 --> 00:16:42.750
and was popularized during
the Harlem Renaissance

00:16:42.750 --> 00:16:46.093
by writers such as James Weldon
Johnson and Nella Larsen.

00:16:47.410 --> 00:16:51.050
Chiaroscuro, high contrast
between dark and light,

00:16:51.050 --> 00:16:53.663
is a common aesthetic
in passing literature.

00:16:55.110 --> 00:16:59.320
Melodramatic sentiments
of fear, taboo, desire,

00:17:06.560 --> 00:17:08.423
and despair predominate.

00:17:10.390 --> 00:17:14.120
It's tragic, sad, and grand.

00:17:14.120 --> 00:17:16.890
And in passing narratives, miscegenation,

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:19.860
usually via the rape of
enslaved black women,

00:17:19.860 --> 00:17:22.660
takes generations to
yield a dramatic change

00:17:22.660 --> 00:17:24.950
in the racial appearance of a family.

00:17:24.950 --> 00:17:28.200
Only then do we find the
melancholy tragic mulatto

00:17:28.200 --> 00:17:30.960
distraught at the injustice
of the one-drop rule

00:17:30.960 --> 00:17:34.060
that banishes them from full subjecthood,

00:17:34.060 --> 00:17:38.593
torn between the white
world and the black world.

00:17:40.050 --> 00:17:44.390
As the word passing
implies, passing is gradual.

00:17:44.390 --> 00:17:48.880
It moves like a wave over
generations, drowning some,

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:52.040
wrecking others on the
reefs of guilt and grief,

00:17:52.040 --> 00:17:54.530
and always moves in one direction,

00:17:54.530 --> 00:17:57.590
toward the foamy crest of whiteness.

00:17:57.590 --> 00:17:59.650
It's a lose-lose game.

00:17:59.650 --> 00:18:02.950
Passing nearly replaces
the social death of racism

00:18:02.950 --> 00:18:05.193
with the social death of isolation.

00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:09.970
It trades the pain of injustice

00:18:09.970 --> 00:18:12.313
for the loss of culture and community.

00:18:13.450 --> 00:18:16.333
The critic, Allyson Hobbs puts it simply,

00:18:18.697 --> 00:18:20.907
"Racial passing is an exile."

00:18:22.430 --> 00:18:26.280
When James Weldon Johnson's
Ex-Colored Man chooses to pass,

00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:31.280
he says, "I have sold my
birthright for a mess of pottage."

00:18:31.400 --> 00:18:32.910
Passing literature can culminate

00:18:32.910 --> 00:18:34.710
with not just a figurative death,

00:18:34.710 --> 00:18:37.320
but a literal passing
over to the other side,

00:18:37.320 --> 00:18:39.140
as in the fate of one character

00:18:39.140 --> 00:18:41.640
in Ella Larsen's 1929 "Passing",

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:43.280
which has just been made into a film

00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:46.820
that's due out next year,
directed by Rebecca Hall.

00:18:46.820 --> 00:18:48.870
And some of you may have seen the preview

00:18:48.870 --> 00:18:50.263
came out just yesterday.

00:18:53.230 --> 00:18:57.340
Despite these perhaps
dark, morbid connotations,

00:18:57.340 --> 00:19:01.020
white Americans soon wanted in on passing.

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:05.410
It portended greater
melancholy, greater melodrama

00:19:05.410 --> 00:19:07.350
than the fakeries of slave impostors

00:19:07.350 --> 00:19:09.980
and the grotesqueries of minstrelsy.

00:19:09.980 --> 00:19:14.530
And so we come to Exhibit
A of our show for today,

00:19:14.530 --> 00:19:17.193
race transformation as morality play.

00:19:18.710 --> 00:19:22.250
In the 1940s, a series
of popular columns ran

00:19:22.250 --> 00:19:25.647
in Negro Digest titled
"If I Were A Negro".

00:19:26.950 --> 00:19:29.310
It had a famous subjunctive entry

00:19:29.310 --> 00:19:32.357
by none other than Eleanor
Roosevelt, who averred,

00:19:32.357 --> 00:19:34.877
"I would have moments
of great bitterness."

00:19:35.770 --> 00:19:37.660
Over the course of the next two decades,

00:19:37.660 --> 00:19:38.790
three white journalists

00:19:38.790 --> 00:19:40.800
would go on to publish their accounts

00:19:40.800 --> 00:19:42.980
of undergoing race transformation

00:19:42.980 --> 00:19:46.483
in order to report on being
a negro from the other side.

00:19:48.150 --> 00:19:51.860
Ray Sprigle's "In the Land
of Jim Crow" From 1949,

00:19:51.860 --> 00:19:55.800
John Howard Griffin's
"Black Like Me" from 1961,

00:19:55.800 --> 00:19:59.163
and Grace Halsell's
"Soul Sister" from 1969.

00:20:00.740 --> 00:20:04.500
At the start of his passing
in reverse, as he calls it,

00:20:04.500 --> 00:20:08.420
Sprigle first applies a
homemade concoction of iodine,

00:20:08.420 --> 00:20:12.790
argyrol, pyrogallic acid,
potassium permanganate,

00:20:12.790 --> 00:20:14.970
and the juice of Walnut hulls.

00:20:14.970 --> 00:20:17.330
This just leaves him striped.

00:20:17.330 --> 00:20:20.140
He finally realizes the
prevalence of light-skin

00:20:20.140 --> 00:20:22.150
among the black population of the US

00:20:22.150 --> 00:20:24.740
and opts just to get a three-week suntan

00:20:24.740 --> 00:20:27.390
before climbing aboard a Jim Crow coach

00:20:27.390 --> 00:20:29.450
on a train headed south.

00:20:29.450 --> 00:20:31.480
Nothing too terrible happens to him,

00:20:31.480 --> 00:20:33.637
which he perversely laments.

00:20:33.637 --> 00:20:35.087
"This would be a far better story

00:20:35.087 --> 00:20:37.477
"if I could show some
scars left by the blackjack

00:20:37.477 --> 00:20:39.927
"of some negro-hating, small town deputy

00:20:39.927 --> 00:20:41.797
"whom I failed to sir.

00:20:41.797 --> 00:20:44.767
"Or a few bullet holes,
mementos of an argument

00:20:44.767 --> 00:20:47.377
"with some trigger-happy
Atlanta motorman."

00:20:48.780 --> 00:20:50.910
A decade later, John Howard Griffin

00:20:50.910 --> 00:20:53.180
begins his scientific research study

00:20:53.180 --> 00:20:54.890
of the negro in the south

00:20:54.890 --> 00:20:57.300
by using a drug for treating vitiligo

00:20:57.300 --> 00:21:01.460
as well as sun stains and a
sunlamp to darken his skin.

00:21:01.460 --> 00:21:04.917
He says, "I went to look
at myself in the mirror,

00:21:04.917 --> 00:21:08.387
"a fierce looking, bald negro
glared at me from the glass.

00:21:08.387 --> 00:21:10.387
"He in no way resembled me.

00:21:10.387 --> 00:21:13.197
"The transformation
was total and shocking.

00:21:13.197 --> 00:21:15.317
"I had expected to see myself disguised,

00:21:15.317 --> 00:21:17.177
"but this was someone else.

00:21:17.177 --> 00:21:20.407
"I was imprisoned in the
flesh of an utter stranger.

00:21:20.407 --> 00:21:22.667
"All traces of the John Griffin I had been

00:21:22.667 --> 00:21:24.647
"were wiped from existence.

00:21:24.647 --> 00:21:27.357
"Even the senses under
when to change so profound

00:21:27.357 --> 00:21:29.407
"it filled me with distress.

00:21:29.407 --> 00:21:32.077
"I looked into the mirror
and saw reflected nothing

00:21:32.077 --> 00:21:34.177
"of the white John Griffin's past.

00:21:34.177 --> 00:21:37.297
"I knew then that I was
not a disguised white man

00:21:37.297 --> 00:21:39.237
"but a newly created negro."

00:21:40.580 --> 00:21:43.910
The race transformation here
is like a gestalt switch

00:21:43.910 --> 00:21:47.140
between obliteration and creation.

00:21:47.140 --> 00:21:50.450
It is apt that the film
version of "Black Like Me"

00:21:50.450 --> 00:21:52.120
ran as a double feature

00:21:52.120 --> 00:21:55.160
with the horror film "The Hands of Orlac"

00:21:55.160 --> 00:21:57.220
in which a concert pianist,

00:21:57.220 --> 00:22:00.080
who receives a hand transplant
from a convicted killer,

00:22:00.080 --> 00:22:02.003
descends into homicidal madness.

00:22:02.960 --> 00:22:07.150
In both films, the physical
transformation isn't a gradual

00:22:07.150 --> 00:22:10.180
or surface matter, but
a wholesale conversion

00:22:10.180 --> 00:22:13.680
at an existential, even ethical level.

00:22:13.680 --> 00:22:15.760
In Griffin's unpublished journals,

00:22:15.760 --> 00:22:17.930
parts of which have now appeared,

00:22:17.930 --> 00:22:20.047
one entry finds him worrying that

00:22:20.047 --> 00:22:22.247
"the physical change
will drag along with it

00:22:22.247 --> 00:22:24.167
"a total transformation of identity,

00:22:24.167 --> 00:22:26.077
"even interior identity."

00:22:26.920 --> 00:22:29.767
Griffin goes on to wonder,
if "becoming a negro

00:22:29.767 --> 00:22:32.767
"will do some profound
damage to my humanity;

00:22:32.767 --> 00:22:33.807
"and still more terrible,

00:22:33.807 --> 00:22:37.640
"will perhaps forever alter
my intimate relationships."

00:22:37.640 --> 00:22:40.410
He even goes on to imagine
having sex with his wife

00:22:40.410 --> 00:22:42.550
while he is black,

00:22:42.550 --> 00:22:46.677
his language teetering between
titillation and horror.

00:22:46.677 --> 00:22:50.087
"I did not ask her if the
idea excited her equally,

00:22:50.087 --> 00:22:51.467
"in this weird situation

00:22:51.467 --> 00:22:54.547
"where intercourse would
be legal, morally okay,

00:22:54.547 --> 00:22:57.267
"but give the illusion of
being a profoundly forbidden

00:22:57.267 --> 00:22:59.257
"and even monstrous act."

00:23:01.170 --> 00:23:03.710
Grace Halsell was mentored by Griffin

00:23:03.710 --> 00:23:07.000
before she began the six-month
stint as a black woman

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:09.640
that she documented in
her book "Soul Sister",

00:23:09.640 --> 00:23:12.533
and later published sketches
of in Ebony magazine.

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:17.410
Halsell takes extreme measures
with her transformation:

00:23:17.410 --> 00:23:19.370
two corrective treatments for vitiligo,

00:23:19.370 --> 00:23:21.150
plus a tanning spell in Mexico,

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:24.140
so intense that she
develops infected blisters

00:23:24.140 --> 00:23:26.490
on her second day in Harlem.

00:23:26.490 --> 00:23:29.720
When a black diner offers to
help her with her ailment,

00:23:29.720 --> 00:23:33.640
she is overcome by the imbalance
of nursing between them.

00:23:33.640 --> 00:23:37.153
It's apparently only supposed
to tilt the other way.

00:23:38.207 --> 00:23:40.777
"I came here to know you for what you are,

00:23:40.777 --> 00:23:44.047
"you beast, you black, black, black man!

00:23:44.047 --> 00:23:47.197
"And you are ugly to me,
and you feel sorry for me.

00:23:47.197 --> 00:23:49.527
"You are pitying me, you
are, Christ in heaven,

00:23:49.527 --> 00:23:50.917
"you are loving me!

00:23:50.917 --> 00:23:52.917
"It is not supposed to be like that!

00:23:52.917 --> 00:23:55.757
"God, how I need you, how I want you."

00:23:56.930 --> 00:24:00.570
Today, these putatively empathic
efforts by white writers

00:24:00.570 --> 00:24:03.670
to understand what it is
like to be black in America

00:24:03.670 --> 00:24:05.560
look like acts of self-serving

00:24:05.560 --> 00:24:08.510
and voyeuristic self-victimization.

00:24:08.510 --> 00:24:11.920
In "Black for a Day: White
Fantasies of Race and Empathy",

00:24:11.920 --> 00:24:14.840
where Alisha Gaines talks
about these three journalists,

00:24:14.840 --> 00:24:17.917
she notes that their "brand of empathy

00:24:17.917 --> 00:24:20.787
"not only refuses to challenge the reality

00:24:20.787 --> 00:24:23.067
"of black second-class citizenship,

00:24:23.067 --> 00:24:25.017
"it actually requires it."

00:24:26.160 --> 00:24:29.440
Gaines also cites bell
hooks' earlier analysis

00:24:29.440 --> 00:24:32.440
of the voracious appetite of white racism,

00:24:32.440 --> 00:24:36.403
which can turn even empathy
into, quote, eating the other.

00:24:37.320 --> 00:24:39.637
In a world of white
supremacy, hooks writes,

00:24:39.637 --> 00:24:43.117
"Desires for the primitive
or fantasies about the other

00:24:43.117 --> 00:24:45.157
"can be continually exploited

00:24:45.157 --> 00:24:48.337
"in a manner that
reinscribes the status quo."

00:24:49.550 --> 00:24:53.180
White racial surrogacy, in
fact, relies on the assumption

00:24:53.180 --> 00:24:56.520
that black suffering is
not legible, not real,

00:24:56.520 --> 00:24:59.180
not worthy of redress
until it is experienced

00:24:59.180 --> 00:25:01.720
and articulated by a white person.

00:25:01.720 --> 00:25:04.927
As Saidiya Hartman puts it
in "Scenes of Subjection",

00:25:04.927 --> 00:25:07.307
"Can the white witness of
the spectacle of suffering

00:25:07.307 --> 00:25:10.257
"affirm the materiality of black sentience

00:25:10.257 --> 00:25:12.807
"only by feeling for himself?"

00:25:14.600 --> 00:25:17.290
The masochism of becoming black

00:25:17.290 --> 00:25:19.290
isn't just a way to assuage white guilt;

00:25:19.290 --> 00:25:22.430
it is also a surreptitious
way to flaunt one's ability

00:25:22.430 --> 00:25:26.810
to survive racism, it's not
so bad if I made it out alive,

00:25:26.810 --> 00:25:30.090
as well as one presumptive
prerogative to "Get Out",

00:25:30.090 --> 00:25:33.670
to invoke Jordan Peele's film,
which I'll discuss later.

00:25:33.670 --> 00:25:37.370
In short, the immediacy,
transience, and opportunism

00:25:37.370 --> 00:25:41.620
of race transformation
encourages a self-congratulatory,

00:25:41.620 --> 00:25:44.900
self-fulfilling return to whiteness.

00:25:44.900 --> 00:25:47.310
In some sense, it's as
though these figures

00:25:47.310 --> 00:25:49.560
need to go across the line

00:25:49.560 --> 00:25:53.503
in order to consolidate
whiteness by returning to it.

00:25:54.360 --> 00:25:55.890
These writers are, in some ways,

00:25:55.890 --> 00:25:57.480
like the minstrel performers who,

00:25:57.480 --> 00:26:00.470
as David Roediger writes
in "Wages of Whiteness,"

00:26:00.470 --> 00:26:03.720
claimed the right to turn black
for as long as they desired

00:26:03.720 --> 00:26:05.593
and to reappear as white.

00:26:06.870 --> 00:26:09.700
These non-fiction memoirs betray a desire

00:26:09.700 --> 00:26:13.050
for the monstrous consequences
of racial surrogacy

00:26:13.050 --> 00:26:14.770
that still relies on being able

00:26:14.770 --> 00:26:17.600
to escape those consequences.

00:26:17.600 --> 00:26:20.650
The wish to expose self-evident racism

00:26:20.650 --> 00:26:24.720
comes to look a lot like
a desire for suffering.

00:26:24.720 --> 00:26:26.790
Halsell breathlessly anticipates

00:26:26.790 --> 00:26:30.167
being raped by both black
men and black women.

00:26:30.167 --> 00:26:33.407
"I went with trembling
heart to the ghetto, Harlem,

00:26:33.407 --> 00:26:35.567
"fearful that a big black bogeyman

00:26:35.567 --> 00:26:37.387
"might tear down the paper-thin door

00:26:37.387 --> 00:26:41.120
"separating my white body
from his lustful desires."

00:26:41.120 --> 00:26:43.140
Alisha Gaines notes the irony

00:26:43.140 --> 00:26:45.170
that Halsell actually quit the project

00:26:45.170 --> 00:26:47.193
when a white man assaulted her.

00:26:48.710 --> 00:26:50.060
Griffin's book evolved

00:26:50.060 --> 00:26:53.110
out of a series in the magazine Sepia,

00:26:53.110 --> 00:26:57.220
which began with a piece
entitled Journey Into Shame,

00:26:57.220 --> 00:27:00.180
implying, of course, a journey back out.

00:27:00.180 --> 00:27:02.770
The pleasure and the penance
of race transformation

00:27:02.770 --> 00:27:04.080
are intertwined.

00:27:04.080 --> 00:27:07.110
The racist white person
secretly yearns to be whipped,

00:27:07.110 --> 00:27:09.800
and what better way than to become black.

00:27:09.800 --> 00:27:13.863
But as Gaines wisely notes
in her title, only for a day.

00:27:15.170 --> 00:27:19.660
The 1986 film "Soul Man"
captures this amalgam

00:27:19.660 --> 00:27:23.460
of resentment, guilt, and entitlement.

00:27:23.460 --> 00:27:26.220
A white student takes tanning pills

00:27:26.220 --> 00:27:28.530
to make himself eligible for a scholarship

00:27:28.530 --> 00:27:31.010
for black students at Harvard Law.

00:27:31.010 --> 00:27:35.820
He falls in love with a
really black single mom

00:27:35.820 --> 00:27:37.310
who was, as it happens,

00:27:37.310 --> 00:27:39.360
the runner up for the scholarship he got.

00:27:40.610 --> 00:27:42.940
The jokes are automatic.

00:27:42.940 --> 00:27:45.163
He plays basketball badly.

00:27:46.260 --> 00:27:48.840
He flirts with stereotypes.

00:27:48.840 --> 00:27:52.400
His preference in music
turns to the soulful.

00:27:52.400 --> 00:27:55.943
He has sex with a white woman,
who exoticizes his penis.

00:27:57.050 --> 00:27:58.760
He eventually confesses,

00:27:58.760 --> 00:28:01.010
promises to donate to civil rights groups,

00:28:01.010 --> 00:28:02.700
punches a couple of racists,

00:28:02.700 --> 00:28:04.360
and is bestowed with this blessing

00:28:04.360 --> 00:28:07.010
from his really black professor.

00:28:07.010 --> 00:28:09.220
You've learned something I can't teach.

00:28:09.220 --> 00:28:12.360
You've learned what it
feels like to be black.

00:28:12.360 --> 00:28:16.920
Our penitent gets the girl,
gets to stay, gets an A minus.

00:28:16.920 --> 00:28:20.380
One can't help but feel that,
as a minor character snarks,

00:28:20.380 --> 00:28:21.870
this whole thing is an argument

00:28:21.870 --> 00:28:24.373
against these affirmative
action scholarships.

00:28:25.360 --> 00:28:28.910
The irony of the penance
arc in race transformation,

00:28:28.910 --> 00:28:32.010
whether inflicted on
the self or on others,

00:28:32.010 --> 00:28:34.520
is that it ends up skewing the equality

00:28:34.520 --> 00:28:37.743
implied by race transformation
in the first place.

00:28:38.740 --> 00:28:41.080
You might think that the upshot,

00:28:41.080 --> 00:28:43.550
as in Dr. Seuss's "The Sneetches",

00:28:43.550 --> 00:28:45.047
would be that the plain-belly sneetches

00:28:45.047 --> 00:28:48.740
and the star-belly sneetches
are the same inside.

00:28:48.740 --> 00:28:51.300
If only the plains and the stars

00:28:51.300 --> 00:28:53.240
could understand each other,

00:28:53.240 --> 00:28:56.860
could understand what it's like
to be in each other's skin,

00:28:56.860 --> 00:28:58.883
everything would be fair.

00:28:59.730 --> 00:29:02.760
But real life is haunted with hidden forms

00:29:02.760 --> 00:29:05.460
of asymmetry and injustice,

00:29:05.460 --> 00:29:09.233
consider the etymological
origin of the word fair.

00:29:10.780 --> 00:29:13.170
Thus, we find the curious fact

00:29:13.170 --> 00:29:15.530
that in all the American
race transformations

00:29:15.530 --> 00:29:19.370
I've encountered, while
both white and black people

00:29:19.370 --> 00:29:24.370
swap skins for pleasure or for privilege,

00:29:24.580 --> 00:29:29.333
I found no black person ever
turned white as a punishment.

00:29:30.610 --> 00:29:32.630
It is somehow inconceivable

00:29:32.630 --> 00:29:36.050
that to become white
could ever be undesirable.

00:29:36.050 --> 00:29:39.020
The obverse implication
is that to be white,

00:29:39.020 --> 00:29:43.010
yet able to turn black at
will, is to have it all.

00:29:43.010 --> 00:29:45.390
You get to be white, you get to be black.

00:29:45.390 --> 00:29:47.840
And if experiencing racism humbles you,

00:29:47.840 --> 00:29:50.863
that's all to the good
too, a moral improvement.

00:29:53.170 --> 00:29:56.170
One of the oddities of the
stories that we've considered

00:29:56.170 --> 00:30:00.740
in Exhibit A are that the stories

00:30:00.740 --> 00:30:04.650
often capitalize on the
language of speculative fiction,

00:30:04.650 --> 00:30:08.053
the Gothic, science fiction, fantasy.

00:30:09.000 --> 00:30:12.950
Sprigle connects his memoir
to a fantastical tradition

00:30:12.950 --> 00:30:16.300
in which the hero wants to
turn, quote, Hindu or Arab

00:30:16.300 --> 00:30:18.380
or one of the other darker races

00:30:18.380 --> 00:30:21.430
and goes to an old woman
and she gives him a lotion

00:30:21.430 --> 00:30:24.030
that turns him dark for weeks or months.

00:30:24.030 --> 00:30:26.870
He finds that being a
black man is as bewildering

00:30:26.870 --> 00:30:29.110
as if I had been dropped on the moon,

00:30:29.110 --> 00:30:30.630
and his fellow white soon become

00:30:30.630 --> 00:30:32.593
a people entirely alien to me.

00:30:33.550 --> 00:30:36.120
For Halsell, it is a
black woman's guest house

00:30:36.120 --> 00:30:37.750
that she finds alien.

00:30:37.750 --> 00:30:39.130
Its residents, she imagines,

00:30:39.130 --> 00:30:42.610
have lived their lives on
one planet, so to speak,

00:30:42.610 --> 00:30:45.130
while she has lived on another.

00:30:45.130 --> 00:30:48.610
Playing black makes her feel
like a spook among spooks,

00:30:48.610 --> 00:30:51.610
disembodied, a cipher floating in a void,

00:30:51.610 --> 00:30:56.020
and a vessel for sorts for
two sorts of personalities,

00:30:56.020 --> 00:30:58.243
two sets of eyes, two bodies.

00:30:59.150 --> 00:31:01.940
This language emphasizes the magic

00:31:01.940 --> 00:31:04.740
and the surrealism of race transformation,

00:31:04.740 --> 00:31:06.950
but also as temporariness.

00:31:06.950 --> 00:31:10.670
The spell can be broken, the charm undone,

00:31:10.670 --> 00:31:15.030
the race transformer, the race transformer

00:31:15.030 --> 00:31:18.530
safely returned to a
utopian no place like home

00:31:18.530 --> 00:31:20.563
from the alien planet that they visited.

00:31:22.010 --> 00:31:23.900
The literary critic, Taylor Evans

00:31:23.900 --> 00:31:25.450
calls the speculative genre,

00:31:25.450 --> 00:31:29.687
that these texts are borrowing
from, techno-passing.

00:31:29.687 --> 00:31:32.887
"Stories that have, as
their technological novum,

00:31:32.887 --> 00:31:35.817
"a fantastical technology
that changes one's appearance

00:31:35.817 --> 00:31:38.367
"from phenotypically white
to phenotypically black

00:31:38.367 --> 00:31:39.757
"or vice versa."

00:31:40.940 --> 00:31:43.870
And with this, I present you Exhibit B,

00:31:43.870 --> 00:31:45.993
speculative race transformation.

00:31:47.390 --> 00:31:49.010
In an archetypal plot,

00:31:49.010 --> 00:31:51.310
you change your race
through some fantastical

00:31:51.310 --> 00:31:52.840
or science fictional gimmick

00:31:52.840 --> 00:31:57.300
that either transforms your
body or transfers your soul

00:31:57.300 --> 00:31:59.423
into a body of another race.

00:32:00.480 --> 00:32:03.810
In these tales, melodrama and minstrelsy

00:32:03.810 --> 00:32:06.913
are fused into a magical presto effect.

00:32:08.480 --> 00:32:12.580
Speculative race transformation
speeds up the melancholy,

00:32:12.580 --> 00:32:16.060
melodramatic longue
duree of racial passing.

00:32:16.060 --> 00:32:19.263
A better term for the genre
would be racial switching.

00:32:20.850 --> 00:32:24.130
The race transformation
is intense, immediate,

00:32:24.130 --> 00:32:25.890
often violent.

00:32:25.890 --> 00:32:30.090
In typical American fashion,
the fact of racial fate,

00:32:30.090 --> 00:32:33.270
the stain or bleach that
results from miscegenation

00:32:33.270 --> 00:32:35.883
is turned into an act of racial will.

00:32:37.520 --> 00:32:40.350
Narrating the ability to
turn your race on or off

00:32:40.350 --> 00:32:41.820
with the flip of a switch

00:32:41.820 --> 00:32:44.820
tends towards certain aesthetic effects.

00:32:44.820 --> 00:32:49.090
Chemical or electric,
catalytic or kinetic energy.

00:32:49.090 --> 00:32:50.710
A compression of time,

00:32:50.710 --> 00:32:54.070
whether that's through
time-travel, identity swapping,

00:32:54.070 --> 00:32:58.733
or simultaneity, or simply
the fact of rapid change.

00:33:01.940 --> 00:33:04.340
We also get an overturning of binaries

00:33:04.340 --> 00:33:06.323
through slapstick and satire.

00:33:07.520 --> 00:33:12.520
The quasi-scientific techniques
that these narratives invent

00:33:12.680 --> 00:33:17.680
make the process of race
transformation quick, topsy-turvy,

00:33:17.800 --> 00:33:20.700
and most importantly reversible,

00:33:20.700 --> 00:33:23.090
both in the sense that you can undo it

00:33:23.090 --> 00:33:26.160
and in the sense that you can
switch in either direction,

00:33:26.160 --> 00:33:28.463
from black to white or white to black.

00:33:29.930 --> 00:33:31.730
Classic works of speculative fiction

00:33:31.730 --> 00:33:34.130
have often flirted with slavery.

00:33:34.130 --> 00:33:38.150
The etymology of the word robot is rabota,

00:33:38.150 --> 00:33:40.123
a Czech word for forced labor.

00:33:42.060 --> 00:33:45.380
We don't have to look too far
outside the familiar cannon

00:33:45.380 --> 00:33:48.440
to find the specific fantasy
of race transformation

00:33:48.440 --> 00:33:50.290
in these texts either.

00:33:50.290 --> 00:33:52.690
In one sequence of Robert Louis Stevenson,

00:33:52.690 --> 00:33:57.690
the "Strange Case of Dr.
Jekyll and Mr. Hyde" from 1887,

00:33:57.920 --> 00:34:00.890
the hero spends the
night carousing, as Hyde,

00:34:00.890 --> 00:34:02.730
then takes an elixir to return

00:34:02.730 --> 00:34:05.370
to his more respectable avatar.

00:34:05.370 --> 00:34:06.630
But in his sleep,

00:34:06.630 --> 00:34:10.260
he unknowingly relapses
into his deviant double

00:34:10.260 --> 00:34:12.663
and winks to see a hand on the bedspread.

00:34:13.777 --> 00:34:16.607
"The hand of Henry Jekyll," he reminds us,

00:34:16.607 --> 00:34:18.867
"was professional in shape and size:

00:34:18.867 --> 00:34:22.327
"it was large, firm, white, and calmly.

00:34:22.327 --> 00:34:24.477
"But the hand which I now saw

00:34:24.477 --> 00:34:28.327
"was lean, corded,
knuckly, of a dusky pallor

00:34:28.327 --> 00:34:31.337
"and thickly shaded with
a swart growth of hair.

00:34:31.337 --> 00:34:34.087
"It was the hand of Edward Hyde."

00:34:35.010 --> 00:34:38.910
Hyde, throughout the novel,
is an amalgam of othernesses.

00:34:38.910 --> 00:34:43.910
He is small, ugly, queer, dwarfish,

00:34:45.450 --> 00:34:48.400
hardly human, ape-like, monstrous,

00:34:51.360 --> 00:34:56.320
a juggernaut, a troglodyte, and a savage.

00:34:56.320 --> 00:34:59.980
The phrase used for the
strange hole that Hyde,

00:34:59.980 --> 00:35:04.980
who's name, no doubt, puns on
animal skin, has over Jekyll,

00:35:05.177 --> 00:35:07.197
"I fell in slavery."

00:35:08.990 --> 00:35:12.150
Witnesses see H.G. Wells' "Invisible Man"

00:35:12.150 --> 00:35:17.150
as black, just blackness, black as my hat,

00:35:18.260 --> 00:35:22.210
but also as a man with a pink
and white face and red eyes,

00:35:22.210 --> 00:35:27.090
and is piebald, black
here and white there,

00:35:27.090 --> 00:35:30.500
a kind of half-breed, whose
colors come off patchy

00:35:30.500 --> 00:35:31.663
instead of mixing.

00:35:33.540 --> 00:35:38.520
The invisible man thinks of
himself as a gaunt black figure,

00:35:38.520 --> 00:35:41.063
and at one point makes this comparison.

00:35:44.170 --> 00:35:46.460
We later learned that he is, in fact,

00:35:46.460 --> 00:35:49.330
white with the whiteness of albinism,

00:35:49.330 --> 00:35:52.857
a condition that facilitates
his transformation.

00:35:52.857 --> 00:35:56.267
"One could make an animal,
a tissue, transparent.

00:35:56.267 --> 00:35:57.947
"One could make it invisible!

00:35:57.947 --> 00:36:00.927
"All except the pigments, I
could be invisible,' I said,

00:36:00.927 --> 00:36:03.837
"suddenly realizing what
it meant to be an albino

00:36:03.837 --> 00:36:05.307
"with such knowledge."

00:36:06.880 --> 00:36:10.010
These quasi-scientific bases
for race transformation

00:36:10.010 --> 00:36:13.980
recur in American pulp
stories of techno-passing.

00:36:13.980 --> 00:36:18.640
Apart from albinism, we
find vitiligo or leukoderma,

00:36:18.640 --> 00:36:19.940
a real medical condition

00:36:19.940 --> 00:36:22.490
in which the skin loses its pigment cells,

00:36:22.490 --> 00:36:26.170
as well as melanosis, a
real biological phenomenon

00:36:26.170 --> 00:36:30.367
in which, as the story
"Pigments is Pigments" puts it,

00:36:30.367 --> 00:36:33.757
"The Arctic fox, the
American varying hare,

00:36:33.757 --> 00:36:36.107
"the ptarmigan, and other mammals

00:36:36.107 --> 00:36:39.547
"always turn black in the
summer, being white or winter."

00:36:40.900 --> 00:36:43.193
David H. Keller's "The Menace,"

00:36:43.193 --> 00:36:47.150
a 1928 four-part novella
published in Amazing Stories,

00:36:47.150 --> 00:36:49.980
pits a humble white detective named Taine

00:36:49.980 --> 00:36:53.140
against a group of elite
power-hungry Harlemites

00:36:53.140 --> 00:36:55.727
called The Powerful Ones.

00:36:55.727 --> 00:36:57.277
"They were all negroes,

00:36:57.277 --> 00:36:59.477
"banded together for the total subjection,

00:36:59.477 --> 00:37:03.400
"and, if necessary, destruction
of the Caucasian race."

00:37:03.400 --> 00:37:06.040
These wicked, quote, white negroes,

00:37:06.040 --> 00:37:07.550
having determined that the difference

00:37:07.550 --> 00:37:08.780
between the negro and the white

00:37:08.780 --> 00:37:10.850
is mainly a matter of pigment,

00:37:10.850 --> 00:37:14.587
invent a serum that
turns black people white.

00:37:14.587 --> 00:37:18.007
"Our physicians inject one drop of serum

00:37:18.007 --> 00:37:19.397
"into the child at birth

00:37:19.397 --> 00:37:22.907
"and any tendency to develop
pigment is at once checked."

00:37:23.780 --> 00:37:26.230
Taine uncovers the Harlemites' plot

00:37:26.230 --> 00:37:29.650
to convert seawater into fake gold,

00:37:29.650 --> 00:37:32.350
and their plan to
infiltrate the water supply

00:37:32.350 --> 00:37:35.343
with a chemical powder that
will turn white people black.

00:37:36.460 --> 00:37:39.170
Taine's success in thwarting
these elaborate plots

00:37:39.170 --> 00:37:41.380
over the course of many, many years

00:37:41.380 --> 00:37:44.840
seems to stem from a natural superiority,

00:37:44.840 --> 00:37:46.930
which the story assumes.

00:37:46.930 --> 00:37:50.500
The white negroes can never
attain true superiority

00:37:50.500 --> 00:37:52.403
no matter their alchemy.

00:37:53.407 --> 00:37:55.757
"Your race can change
the color of their skins

00:37:55.757 --> 00:37:58.467
"but they cannot change
the color of their souls.

00:37:58.467 --> 00:38:00.147
"No matter how white they may become,

00:38:00.147 --> 00:38:02.767
"they will always remain black inside."

00:38:03.910 --> 00:38:06.590
In the 1930 story "Dr. Immortelle",

00:38:06.590 --> 00:38:09.260
also published in Amazing Stories,

00:38:09.260 --> 00:38:13.117
the narrator has undergone
an experiment in immortality.

00:38:13.117 --> 00:38:15.447
"The gradual loss of most of my own blood

00:38:15.447 --> 00:38:17.887
"and the substitution of Caucasian blood

00:38:17.887 --> 00:38:20.770
"through the process of transfusion."

00:38:20.770 --> 00:38:24.600
Blood becomes a literal vehicle
for race transformation,

00:38:24.600 --> 00:38:26.770
but also a metaphorical one for a set

00:38:26.770 --> 00:38:30.910
of familiar eugenic assumptions
about, as the story puts it,

00:38:30.910 --> 00:38:33.857
the "negro race being
inferior to the Caucasian."

00:38:34.730 --> 00:38:37.131
A white love interest
in the story has, quote,

00:38:37.131 --> 00:38:41.270
a lily-white soul, while
the formerly enslaved black,

00:38:41.270 --> 00:38:46.200
Dr. Immortelle, has a soul
as black as my face once was.

00:38:46.200 --> 00:38:49.260
He also has a passionate
desire for offspring

00:38:49.260 --> 00:38:53.480
and a tendency toward inertia
all because he still retains

00:38:53.480 --> 00:38:56.223
a drop of negroid blood in his veins.

00:38:57.490 --> 00:39:00.680
Swapping a soul into the
body of a different race

00:39:00.680 --> 00:39:03.320
is a device called identity transfer.

00:39:03.320 --> 00:39:05.630
And it's a common science
fictional mechanism

00:39:05.630 --> 00:39:07.410
for race transformation.

00:39:07.410 --> 00:39:09.787
This is the basis for
Harry Stephen Keeler's

00:39:09.787 --> 00:39:12.810
posthumously published "The
Man Who Changed His Skin",

00:39:12.810 --> 00:39:14.923
which was originally written in the '30s.

00:39:16.230 --> 00:39:19.100
In this novel, a 19th
century white Bostonian

00:39:19.100 --> 00:39:22.680
receives a jet black pallet of oguva gum

00:39:22.680 --> 00:39:24.650
from a homeless man.

00:39:24.650 --> 00:39:27.080
The homeless man claims he
got it from an African tree

00:39:27.080 --> 00:39:31.190
that sprouted from a celestial
seed planted by a meteor.

00:39:31.190 --> 00:39:34.580
If you swallow it, he says,
your soul, spirit and mind

00:39:34.580 --> 00:39:38.470
will slide off into the
carcass of somebody else.

00:39:38.470 --> 00:39:40.990
The Bostonian takes the
pellet and wakes up,

00:39:40.990 --> 00:39:43.330
like Dr. Jekyll, to an unexpected site

00:39:43.330 --> 00:39:45.567
at the end of his sleeve.

00:39:45.567 --> 00:39:47.917
"The hand was black as coal.

00:39:47.917 --> 00:39:52.917
"My God, I am somebody
other than who I was."

00:39:53.890 --> 00:39:56.330
Over time these science
fictional procedures

00:39:56.330 --> 00:39:58.650
seem to grow more sophisticated.

00:39:58.650 --> 00:40:00.940
Identity transfer becomes literalized

00:40:00.940 --> 00:40:03.450
in the form of brain transplants,

00:40:03.450 --> 00:40:07.550
as in the 1969 film "Change of Mind".

00:40:07.550 --> 00:40:09.180
To investigate a murder,

00:40:09.180 --> 00:40:12.860
a white lawyer's brain is placed
in the body of a black man.

00:40:12.860 --> 00:40:16.750
The tagline asks, is he a white
man with a black man's body

00:40:16.750 --> 00:40:19.283
or a black man with a white man's brain?

00:40:21.410 --> 00:40:26.410
In Jess Row's 2014 novel,
"Your Face in Mine",

00:40:26.510 --> 00:40:29.100
not just the skin, but other features too

00:40:29.100 --> 00:40:31.900
are transformed through what's called

00:40:31.900 --> 00:40:34.500
racial reassignment surgery,

00:40:34.500 --> 00:40:36.820
so named to emphasize its resonance

00:40:36.820 --> 00:40:38.863
with sexual reassignment surgery.

00:40:39.940 --> 00:40:41.690
A man who claims to have had

00:40:41.690 --> 00:40:44.907
racial identity dysphoria undergoes

00:40:44.907 --> 00:40:46.587
"a series of facial surgeries,

00:40:46.587 --> 00:40:49.297
"scalp surgeries,
body-sculpting procedures,

00:40:49.297 --> 00:40:51.147
"and pigmentation treatments,

00:40:51.147 --> 00:40:53.257
"transforming me from
my original appearance

00:40:53.257 --> 00:40:55.357
"as a Caucasian-Jewish white male

00:40:55.357 --> 00:40:57.717
"into a convincing African-American."

00:40:59.090 --> 00:41:00.530
While the scientific techniques

00:41:00.530 --> 00:41:04.020
for race transformation in
these texts seem to advance,

00:41:04.020 --> 00:41:06.970
the stories remain stuck
in an old mysticism

00:41:06.970 --> 00:41:08.770
of racial essentialism:

00:41:08.770 --> 00:41:11.210
the idea that black and white features

00:41:11.210 --> 00:41:14.883
carry inherent values like
beauty or intelligence.

00:41:17.080 --> 00:41:19.030
Essentialism also gives us the feeling

00:41:19.030 --> 00:41:21.050
that race subsists in the soul

00:41:21.050 --> 00:41:24.263
and that people can be
born in the wrong race.

00:41:25.320 --> 00:41:28.240
These contradictions
should be familiar to you

00:41:28.240 --> 00:41:30.273
from American racial politics.

00:41:31.850 --> 00:41:36.103
Race in America is somehow
both surface and depth,

00:41:36.960 --> 00:41:39.690
construct and essence,

00:41:39.690 --> 00:41:42.620
transmutable yet fixed,

00:41:42.620 --> 00:41:46.100
a contingent phenomenon
with manifest effects,

00:41:46.100 --> 00:41:48.313
a fantasy and a reality.

00:41:49.400 --> 00:41:52.550
It's a chess game that insists
on an equal distribution

00:41:52.550 --> 00:41:54.140
of black and white elements,

00:41:54.140 --> 00:41:56.853
but in which white just
happens to go first.

00:41:57.950 --> 00:42:01.240
The paradoxical sense that
some magic has bestowed

00:42:01.240 --> 00:42:05.020
incalculable value upon
something with no intrinsic worth

00:42:05.020 --> 00:42:06.900
is hardly strange, though.

00:42:06.900 --> 00:42:07.990
We engage with something

00:42:07.990 --> 00:42:11.523
that fits this description
every day: money.

00:42:12.790 --> 00:42:16.750
Capitalism's magic
transforms coins and cash,

00:42:16.750 --> 00:42:20.280
making them both fungible and abstract,

00:42:20.280 --> 00:42:23.670
indices of the values of other things.

00:42:23.670 --> 00:42:27.170
This isn't a mere analogy
between money and race.

00:42:27.170 --> 00:42:29.330
Money gleams and rustles

00:42:29.330 --> 00:42:32.080
throughout race transformation tales.

00:42:32.080 --> 00:42:35.130
The "Ex-Colored Man"
who sold his birthright

00:42:35.130 --> 00:42:38.550
when he chose to pass,
whereas a $10 gold coin

00:42:38.550 --> 00:42:41.090
that his white father bequeathed to him.

00:42:41.090 --> 00:42:43.450
In "The Menace", the evil Harlemites

00:42:43.450 --> 00:42:45.720
alchemized black to
white and white to black,

00:42:45.720 --> 00:42:48.830
but also seawater to false gold.

00:42:48.830 --> 00:42:51.940
And in "Pigments is Pigments"
a malevolent millionaire

00:42:51.940 --> 00:42:54.690
is turned from white to coal, ebony black,

00:42:54.690 --> 00:42:58.400
and then from black to a
white as alabaster albino

00:42:58.400 --> 00:42:59.770
by a biologist.

00:42:59.770 --> 00:43:02.123
Why, because he scammed him out of money.

00:43:03.270 --> 00:43:07.513
These stories know that,
in America, race is money.

00:43:08.670 --> 00:43:12.810
If one race, black people,
was essentially invented

00:43:12.810 --> 00:43:17.620
in order to be bought, sold,
used and enjoyed at will,

00:43:17.620 --> 00:43:20.810
the desire to transform
oneself into that race,

00:43:20.810 --> 00:43:22.520
even imaginatively,

00:43:22.520 --> 00:43:25.840
amounts to the capitalist impulse to own.

00:43:25.840 --> 00:43:28.637
As Toni Morrison notes
in "Playing in the Dark",

00:43:28.637 --> 00:43:32.037
"The save population, it
could be and was assumed,

00:43:32.037 --> 00:43:35.490
"offered itself as surrogate selves."

00:43:35.490 --> 00:43:39.070
Black people have so long
been deemed to be property

00:43:39.070 --> 00:43:41.430
that to put race on and take it off

00:43:41.430 --> 00:43:44.070
becomes akin to consumerism.

00:43:44.070 --> 00:43:46.140
This is clear when it comes to

00:43:46.140 --> 00:43:49.580
the contemporary techno-minstrelsy
of black fishing,

00:43:49.580 --> 00:43:52.980
which is the phenomenon of
non-black people using bronzer,

00:43:52.980 --> 00:43:57.190
tanning, photoshop, and/or
cosmetic surgery to appear black,

00:43:57.190 --> 00:43:58.723
especially on social media.

00:44:00.200 --> 00:44:01.470
Now, some might claim

00:44:01.470 --> 00:44:04.250
that this desire to cosplay another race

00:44:04.250 --> 00:44:07.140
is an innocent form of allophilia,

00:44:07.140 --> 00:44:08.710
an affinity for otherness

00:44:08.710 --> 00:44:11.710
or liking people who
are different from you.

00:44:11.710 --> 00:44:14.590
But race transformation
tales are always grounded

00:44:14.590 --> 00:44:16.450
in familiar stereotypes,

00:44:16.450 --> 00:44:20.530
and this begs the question
of otherness, of diversity.

00:44:20.530 --> 00:44:24.700
In short, racial otherness
somehow always looks the same.

00:44:24.700 --> 00:44:27.343
It is a brand you wear, so to speak.

00:44:28.430 --> 00:44:31.107
And so we find that the value of whiteness

00:44:31.107 --> 00:44:34.960
and race transformation tales
always seems to manifest

00:44:34.960 --> 00:44:37.340
in abstract aspects of personhood

00:44:37.340 --> 00:44:40.653
like safety, status, and purchasing power.

00:44:41.860 --> 00:44:46.150
The value of blackness by
contrast has long been cast

00:44:46.150 --> 00:44:50.123
as physical, as sensual, even bestial.

00:44:51.140 --> 00:44:54.180
For some reason, the two
Disney animated movies

00:44:54.180 --> 00:44:56.017
that feature black protagonists,

00:44:56.017 --> 00:44:59.090
"The Princess and the Frog" and "Soul"

00:44:59.090 --> 00:45:03.260
magically place both of those
protagonists inside an animal

00:45:03.260 --> 00:45:04.553
for much of the plot.

00:45:06.060 --> 00:45:09.860
A disconcerting number of race
transformation tales intimate

00:45:09.860 --> 00:45:12.560
that being a black man is
correlated with having a large

00:45:12.560 --> 00:45:15.100
and skillful penis, an erotic obsession

00:45:15.100 --> 00:45:18.140
that is tinged with fear and resentment.

00:45:18.140 --> 00:45:20.650
It's just as often implied
that becoming black

00:45:20.650 --> 00:45:24.510
bestows the ability to dance,
to play or appreciate music,

00:45:24.510 --> 00:45:26.580
and to excel at sports.

00:45:26.580 --> 00:45:29.100
In a 2005 episode of "South Park",

00:45:29.100 --> 00:45:31.950
Kyle has a, quote, negroplasty

00:45:31.950 --> 00:45:33.943
to make him better at basketball.

00:45:35.370 --> 00:45:38.240
It makes sense that in the
race transformation fantasies

00:45:38.240 --> 00:45:40.030
I've exhibited so far,

00:45:40.030 --> 00:45:42.610
all of which were
created by white authors,

00:45:42.610 --> 00:45:45.330
race is seen as a kind of property,

00:45:45.330 --> 00:45:47.083
in several senses of the word.

00:45:48.340 --> 00:45:51.520
First, race is a bodily property,

00:45:51.520 --> 00:45:54.220
a set of features affixed to the body

00:45:54.220 --> 00:45:55.520
that require great labor

00:45:55.520 --> 00:45:58.213
or the energy of magic to transform.

00:45:59.320 --> 00:46:01.940
Second, race is assumed to be attached

00:46:01.940 --> 00:46:03.730
to the individual subject,

00:46:03.730 --> 00:46:06.140
to their so-called proper self,

00:46:06.140 --> 00:46:09.650
whose relationship to the
property of land or wealth

00:46:09.650 --> 00:46:11.793
is often determined by their race.

00:46:12.670 --> 00:46:15.170
And third, race can be owned.

00:46:15.170 --> 00:46:18.540
It is itself property
to be bought or sold.

00:46:18.540 --> 00:46:21.560
It can be pegged to a
market value like gold.

00:46:21.560 --> 00:46:22.860
It has a form of capital

00:46:22.860 --> 00:46:26.433
that is seen as fungible and
abstract, but can be hoarded.

00:46:27.800 --> 00:46:29.680
Speculative race transformation stories,

00:46:29.680 --> 00:46:32.360
thus, offer evidence for the argument

00:46:32.360 --> 00:46:35.893
that the legal scholar,
Cheryl I. Harris made in 1993.

00:46:36.847 --> 00:46:38.597
"Whiteness, initially constructed

00:46:38.597 --> 00:46:40.437
"as a form of racial identity,

00:46:40.437 --> 00:46:42.757
"evolved into a form of property,

00:46:42.757 --> 00:46:44.587
"historically and presently acknowledged

00:46:44.587 --> 00:46:46.887
"and protected in American law.

00:46:46.887 --> 00:46:49.427
"Following the period
of slavery and conquest,

00:46:49.427 --> 00:46:52.397
"whiteness became the basis
of racialized privilege,

00:46:52.397 --> 00:46:54.997
"a type of status in which
white racial identity

00:46:54.997 --> 00:46:59.047
"provided the basis for
allocating societal benefits

00:46:59.047 --> 00:47:01.687
"both private and public in character.

00:47:01.687 --> 00:47:05.367
"These arrangements were
ratified and legitimated in law

00:47:05.367 --> 00:47:07.247
"as a type of status property."

00:47:08.300 --> 00:47:10.530
Race is not strictly arbitrary

00:47:10.530 --> 00:47:13.240
but it has always been
subject to arbitration,

00:47:13.240 --> 00:47:15.633
contingent on the law in America.

00:47:17.360 --> 00:47:20.010
We can see all of these racial logics,

00:47:20.010 --> 00:47:25.010
passing, prurients,
perversity, and property,

00:47:26.020 --> 00:47:28.090
come together in a recent specimen

00:47:28.090 --> 00:47:30.830
of speculative race transformation.

00:47:30.830 --> 00:47:34.050
Matt Ruff's 2016 book "Lovecraft Country"

00:47:34.050 --> 00:47:37.330
entwines a history of the Jim Crow 1950s

00:47:37.330 --> 00:47:39.520
with a pulp subtext.

00:47:39.520 --> 00:47:42.800
In a chapter called Jekyll and Hyde Park,

00:47:42.800 --> 00:47:46.320
Ruff revisits and reverses
Robert Louis Stevenson's

00:47:46.320 --> 00:47:48.023
race transformation scene.

00:47:48.990 --> 00:47:51.360
A woman named Ruby, curvy and dark,

00:47:51.360 --> 00:47:53.180
wakes in an unfamiliar bed,

00:47:53.180 --> 00:47:56.050
on what appeared to be blood slick sheets.

00:47:56.050 --> 00:47:58.510
In terror, she stumbled to a bathroom,

00:47:58.510 --> 00:48:02.540
splashes water on herself,
then opens her eyes to see,

00:48:02.540 --> 00:48:05.327
quote, "a crazed white woman's
face hovering in the dark

00:48:05.327 --> 00:48:07.287
"just inches from her own.

00:48:07.287 --> 00:48:08.637
"Ruby screamed.

00:48:08.637 --> 00:48:10.737
"The white woman in the
mirror screamed with her.

00:48:10.737 --> 00:48:12.957
"Ruby clapped her hands over her mouth;

00:48:12.957 --> 00:48:14.997
"the white woman aped the gesture.

00:48:14.997 --> 00:48:16.787
"The white woman: her."

00:48:18.180 --> 00:48:23.180
The 2020 HBO adaptation of
the book "Lovecraft Country"

00:48:23.510 --> 00:48:28.510
repeats the sequence, but
goes on to mix its messages,

00:48:28.940 --> 00:48:32.360
adopting a radical narrative logic

00:48:32.360 --> 00:48:35.893
that yet runs up against a
stereotyped visual logic.

00:48:38.050 --> 00:48:41.263
Race, as in "Dr.
Immortelle", is in the blood.

00:48:42.620 --> 00:48:46.460
In the novel, blood seeps
up under Ruby's nails

00:48:46.460 --> 00:48:49.500
whenever she starts to slip back to black.

00:48:49.500 --> 00:48:51.620
A white woman's blood is a key ingredient

00:48:51.620 --> 00:48:53.970
of the elixir that transforms her.

00:48:53.970 --> 00:48:56.570
In the show, the blood is thicker,

00:48:56.570 --> 00:48:58.890
more literal, more gruesome.

00:48:58.890 --> 00:49:01.320
Indeed, Ruby doesn't turn white

00:49:01.320 --> 00:49:04.500
so much as she wears a tight
white flesh suit inside,

00:49:04.500 --> 00:49:06.620
which she shimmies seductively,

00:49:06.620 --> 00:49:10.530
dancing being in her blood
than seems to burst out of,

00:49:10.530 --> 00:49:13.400
her real body being naturally curvy

00:49:13.400 --> 00:49:18.400
and eventually rips off in
dripping strips and chunks.

00:49:18.800 --> 00:49:22.550
To transform your race
comes to seem laborious,

00:49:22.550 --> 00:49:26.710
perverse, grotesque, as perhaps it should;

00:49:26.710 --> 00:49:28.800
but it also comes to seem vengeful.

00:49:28.800 --> 00:49:30.600
In the scene I just showed you,

00:49:30.600 --> 00:49:33.170
Ruby starts out wearing a white skin

00:49:33.170 --> 00:49:35.270
in order to apply for a dream job,

00:49:35.270 --> 00:49:39.320
but winds up tying up her
racist, handsy white manager

00:49:39.320 --> 00:49:42.203
and then murdering him with a stiletto.

00:49:44.150 --> 00:49:47.390
The white skin of
Hillary, that Ruby adopts,

00:49:47.390 --> 00:49:51.500
is essentially a temporary
disguise that grants her access,

00:49:51.500 --> 00:49:55.270
or perhaps permission, to
release some inner savagery,

00:49:55.270 --> 00:49:57.343
which is here marked as black.

00:49:58.300 --> 00:50:01.050
The contradiction at the
core of race transformation

00:50:01.050 --> 00:50:04.550
erupts as a clash between
the show's narrative logic,

00:50:04.550 --> 00:50:06.640
race can be swapped at will;

00:50:06.640 --> 00:50:09.770
and its visual logic,
where blackness is violent,

00:50:09.770 --> 00:50:11.393
whiteness is delicate.

00:50:14.530 --> 00:50:17.510
In writing his novel, Ruff said,

00:50:17.510 --> 00:50:20.410
he wished to redress the
troubling history of race

00:50:20.410 --> 00:50:22.237
in genre fiction.

00:50:22.237 --> 00:50:25.057
"One of the many forms of
exclusion African Americans faced

00:50:25.057 --> 00:50:27.937
"was being shut out of
the popular imagination.

00:50:27.937 --> 00:50:29.997
"For as long as genre fiction has existed,

00:50:29.997 --> 00:50:32.317
"there have been black genre fiction fans,

00:50:32.317 --> 00:50:35.920
"but most of the time they were
either ignored or insulted."

00:50:35.920 --> 00:50:38.347
He says that he wanted
to give black people

00:50:38.347 --> 00:50:41.747
"a chance to start in their
own personal weird tale.

00:50:41.747 --> 00:50:45.537
"Of course, tales of the
supernatural are just a lot of fun.

00:50:45.537 --> 00:50:47.057
"There's a reason African Americans

00:50:47.057 --> 00:50:49.500
"wanted a chance to play, too."

00:50:49.500 --> 00:50:51.900
Misha Green, the black showrunner

00:50:51.900 --> 00:50:54.447
of the HBO adaptation concurs,

00:50:54.447 --> 00:50:58.417
"What Matt was doing in the
book was reclaiming genre spaces

00:50:58.417 --> 00:51:01.117
"for people who have typically
been left out of them."

00:51:02.260 --> 00:51:05.750
These well-meaning creators
forget, or perhaps don't know,

00:51:05.750 --> 00:51:08.270
that while black people
may have been denied

00:51:08.270 --> 00:51:11.640
the artistic privileges
afforded to white people,

00:51:11.640 --> 00:51:14.890
blackness has long been the
ground of American music,

00:51:14.890 --> 00:51:16.180
film and literature.

00:51:16.180 --> 00:51:19.380
They've always been playing too.

00:51:19.380 --> 00:51:22.327
As Morrison writes,
"Black slavery enriched

00:51:22.327 --> 00:51:24.337
"the country's creative possibilities.

00:51:24.337 --> 00:51:27.857
"The result was a playground
for the imagination."

00:51:29.000 --> 00:51:33.530
A 21st century liberal
rhetoric of inclusion

00:51:33.530 --> 00:51:37.800
ends up centering whiteness as
a kind of historical default,

00:51:37.800 --> 00:51:39.313
included by whom?

00:51:40.200 --> 00:51:42.870
But it only does through an erasure

00:51:42.870 --> 00:51:44.880
of this cultural history.

00:51:44.880 --> 00:51:47.070
It doesn't seem to
recognize, for instance,

00:51:47.070 --> 00:51:50.610
that black people have long
been writing their own weird

00:51:50.610 --> 00:51:52.303
and supernatural tales.

00:51:53.170 --> 00:51:56.640
Indeed, black writers
invented race transformation,

00:51:56.640 --> 00:51:58.970
not just before Matt Ruff,

00:51:58.970 --> 00:52:03.460
but before Robert Louis
Stevenson even took up his pen.

00:52:03.460 --> 00:52:06.693
And so we come to our
final event, Exhibit C,

00:52:07.770 --> 00:52:11.180
black speculative race transformation

00:52:12.820 --> 00:52:15.190
Hannah Crafts' "The Bondwoman's Narrative"

00:52:15.190 --> 00:52:17.370
has been authenticated as the first novel

00:52:17.370 --> 00:52:20.050
written by an African American woman.

00:52:20.050 --> 00:52:22.260
It was first published in 2002,

00:52:22.260 --> 00:52:25.300
but was likely written in the 1850s.

00:52:25.300 --> 00:52:27.290
It includes a highly amusing scene

00:52:27.290 --> 00:52:29.270
in which a black enslaved woman,

00:52:29.270 --> 00:52:32.010
herself so light-skinned
that she can pass,

00:52:32.010 --> 00:52:34.670
tricks her putatively white mistress

00:52:34.670 --> 00:52:37.160
into applying an Italian face powder

00:52:37.160 --> 00:52:39.593
that briefly turns the skin black.

00:52:42.220 --> 00:52:43.790
The powder certainly is white,

00:52:43.790 --> 00:52:45.900
and yet it may possess
such chemical properties

00:52:45.900 --> 00:52:47.750
as a Cajun blackness.

00:52:47.750 --> 00:52:49.767
Indeed, "I recently saw in the newspapers

00:52:49.767 --> 00:52:50.877
"some accounts of a chemist

00:52:50.877 --> 00:52:52.427
"who having been jilted by a lady

00:52:52.427 --> 00:52:55.767
"very liberal in the application
of powder to her face

00:52:55.767 --> 00:52:58.107
"had invented as a method of revenge

00:52:58.107 --> 00:52:59.707
"a certain kind of smelling bottles,

00:52:59.707 --> 00:53:02.807
"of which the fumes would
suddenly blacken the whitest skin

00:53:02.807 --> 00:53:05.827
"provided the said cosmetic
had been previously applied."

00:53:06.750 --> 00:53:10.190
Professor Gates identified two
scientific American articles

00:53:10.190 --> 00:53:13.010
to which Hannah Crafts
would've had access,

00:53:13.010 --> 00:53:16.350
both of which mentioned a
white silver nitrate powder

00:53:16.350 --> 00:53:19.163
that in the presence
of ammonia turns black.

00:53:21.400 --> 00:53:23.980
It turns out that one of our
most important sociologists

00:53:23.980 --> 00:53:24.980
and theorists have raised,

00:53:24.980 --> 00:53:27.170
none other than W.E.B. Du Bois,

00:53:27.170 --> 00:53:29.920
also drafted a race transformation tale

00:53:29.920 --> 00:53:32.943
when he was an undergraduate
at Harvard in 1889.

00:53:34.420 --> 00:53:39.420
The story's narrator, a kind
of hustler, tells a white man,

00:53:40.237 --> 00:53:43.117
"After a painless operation
performed by one of my friends

00:53:43.117 --> 00:53:45.957
"you will appear as a
negro, a full-fledged darky;

00:53:45.957 --> 00:53:48.117
"we will then set out
through the land of the free

00:53:48.117 --> 00:53:50.360
"and the home of the brave."

00:53:50.360 --> 00:53:52.620
After the transformation, he's told,

00:53:52.620 --> 00:53:55.697
your "Anglo-Saxon lips of the
pretty red pouting variety

00:53:55.697 --> 00:53:57.467
"have gained thickness with color,

00:53:57.467 --> 00:54:00.157
"your hair with becoming
modesty has slunk back,

00:54:00.157 --> 00:54:02.667
"cringing, ashamed either of the owner

00:54:02.667 --> 00:54:04.467
"or of its owner's owners,

00:54:04.467 --> 00:54:06.727
"and your countenance which
before the application

00:54:06.727 --> 00:54:09.217
"of prepared lampblack
was open and winning

00:54:09.217 --> 00:54:12.327
"has suddenly become to
the last degree repulsive.

00:54:12.327 --> 00:54:13.387
"Presto!"

00:54:14.350 --> 00:54:17.220
The temporary transformation
exposes this white student

00:54:17.220 --> 00:54:19.270
to the condescension and derision

00:54:19.270 --> 00:54:21.280
of Harvard faculty, students and staff,

00:54:21.280 --> 00:54:24.650
which presumably Dubois was
experiencing at the time.

00:54:24.650 --> 00:54:27.100
Yet there are advantages
into this so-called

00:54:27.100 --> 00:54:30.957
vacation tide lark until
the negro point of view.

00:54:30.957 --> 00:54:33.247
"You step into a new and, to most people,

00:54:33.247 --> 00:54:36.027
"entirely unknown region of the universe.

00:54:36.027 --> 00:54:39.017
"You will be in a position
to solve in a measure

00:54:39.017 --> 00:54:43.247
"the problems of introspection
and the fourth dimension."

00:54:44.520 --> 00:54:47.220
In this early speculative satire,

00:54:47.220 --> 00:54:49.770
being black offers a double consciousness,

00:54:49.770 --> 00:54:54.283
a second site that is not only
social but seems existential.

00:54:55.400 --> 00:54:58.890
Another barely studied
text by a black author,

00:54:58.890 --> 00:55:01.980
there are only 22 extent
copies in the world,

00:55:01.980 --> 00:55:05.427
is Robert Gilbert Wells'
self-published 1905

00:55:05.427 --> 00:55:08.537
"Anthropology Applied to the
American White Man and Negro."

00:55:09.574 --> 00:55:10.800
And this text is extraordinary.

00:55:10.800 --> 00:55:14.390
It includes collages, and photographs,

00:55:14.390 --> 00:55:18.010
as well as a scene of race transformation.

00:55:18.010 --> 00:55:21.640
A formerly enslaved man
named Sam uses a potion

00:55:21.640 --> 00:55:25.493
to turn his white former
master, Mr. Jones black.

00:55:26.790 --> 00:55:31.467
And that the image on the left
is precisely of that moment.

00:55:31.467 --> 00:55:34.127
"By the time he had caught his breath,

00:55:34.127 --> 00:55:36.417
"he was out of the door
of the white man's coach

00:55:36.417 --> 00:55:39.187
"and thrown head long into
the negro's Jim Crow car,

00:55:39.187 --> 00:55:40.967
"mangled and bleeding.

00:55:40.967 --> 00:55:42.247
"Sam had heard the racket

00:55:42.247 --> 00:55:44.277
"and was watching what is commonly known

00:55:44.277 --> 00:55:46.017
"as the white man's fun and method

00:55:46.017 --> 00:55:48.770
"of teaching the negro his place."

00:55:48.770 --> 00:55:51.340
After the black and Mr.
Jones is almost lynched

00:55:51.340 --> 00:55:54.110
for trying to talk to his
white wife and family,

00:55:54.110 --> 00:55:58.053
Sam uses fluids and liquids
to reverse the transformation.

00:55:59.380 --> 00:56:03.240
In Charles Chesnutt story
"Mars Jeems's Nightmare",

00:56:03.240 --> 00:56:06.140
Mars Jeems is a hard and cruel man,

00:56:06.140 --> 00:56:08.270
monstrous trick with his hands.

00:56:08.270 --> 00:56:11.300
The hands on his plantation
seek help from Aunt Peggy,

00:56:11.300 --> 00:56:13.840
the conjure woman, and
she gives them a goofer

00:56:13.840 --> 00:56:15.630
made of powdered roots.

00:56:15.630 --> 00:56:17.180
It turns Mars Jeems black

00:56:17.180 --> 00:56:19.480
and he winds up at his own plantation,

00:56:19.480 --> 00:56:22.380
supposedly as payment for a gambling debt.

00:56:22.380 --> 00:56:24.120
A poor white overseer whips

00:56:24.120 --> 00:56:27.203
and abuses the new slave mercilessly.

00:56:28.340 --> 00:56:31.730
When a hexed sweet potato
turns Jeems white again,

00:56:31.730 --> 00:56:33.600
he resumed as authority as master,

00:56:33.600 --> 00:56:36.850
but fires that racist
overseer, relaxes his rules

00:56:36.850 --> 00:56:39.283
and ends up with a finer plantation.

00:56:40.420 --> 00:56:44.130
So we see again the tendency
toward pedagogical pennants

00:56:44.130 --> 00:56:45.760
for the white racist.

00:56:45.760 --> 00:56:48.010
But the aim of the satire in these texts

00:56:48.010 --> 00:56:51.910
is not to rest an eye for an
eye, but rather to open eyes,

00:56:51.910 --> 00:56:54.360
make people see the world
through the polarized view

00:56:54.360 --> 00:56:55.773
of double consciousness.

00:56:56.930 --> 00:57:01.310
In George Schuyler's 1931
satire "Black No More",

00:57:01.310 --> 00:57:03.520
Max Disher, fresh from sexual rejection,

00:57:03.520 --> 00:57:05.400
from both his high yellow girlfriend

00:57:05.400 --> 00:57:07.090
and a white woman in a nightclub,

00:57:07.090 --> 00:57:09.673
stumbles upon the black-no-more procedure.

00:57:10.530 --> 00:57:14.950
This device uses electrical
nutrition and glandular control

00:57:14.950 --> 00:57:17.450
to induce and speed up vitiligo,

00:57:17.450 --> 00:57:20.497
which, per the novel,
"absolutely removes skin pigment

00:57:20.497 --> 00:57:23.170
"and turns a negro completely white."

00:57:23.170 --> 00:57:25.200
Enough people use the black-no-more

00:57:25.200 --> 00:57:28.310
that American social
hierarchy is overturned.

00:57:28.310 --> 00:57:29.910
Leaders of black communities

00:57:29.910 --> 00:57:32.980
and white supremacist
organizations both riot,

00:57:32.980 --> 00:57:36.610
politicians are rousted
and institutions raised.

00:57:36.610 --> 00:57:40.210
To be white becomes a sure sign
you were once really black.

00:57:40.210 --> 00:57:42.870
Newborns and genealogy
charts are scrutinized

00:57:42.870 --> 00:57:44.310
for any hint of blackness,

00:57:44.310 --> 00:57:47.830
which becomes a matter of
descent rather than appearance.

00:57:47.830 --> 00:57:50.140
Black-no-more soon tumbles into a jumbling

00:57:50.140 --> 00:57:53.270
of too many shades to
keep track of or to rank.

00:57:53.270 --> 00:57:55.590
Soon enough, a proto tanning lotion

00:57:55.590 --> 00:57:58.640
called an Egyptienne stain
becomes a bestseller,

00:57:58.640 --> 00:58:01.573
and the most popular tint is the Zulu tan.

00:58:03.010 --> 00:58:05.190
An instructed juxtaposition is evident

00:58:05.190 --> 00:58:09.007
in the production history of
the 1970 film "Watermelon Man".

00:58:10.110 --> 00:58:12.080
In Herman Raucher's script,

00:58:12.080 --> 00:58:16.540
a racist white insurance
salesman wakes up black.

00:58:16.540 --> 00:58:20.570
Hysteria ensues, screams, tears, mobs.

00:58:20.570 --> 00:58:24.070
He desperately tries to blanch
his skin with floods of milk,

00:58:24.070 --> 00:58:25.850
soap and bleach.

00:58:25.850 --> 00:58:28.380
His liberal wife stays level-headed.

00:58:28.380 --> 00:58:29.340
It would serve you right

00:58:29.340 --> 00:58:31.900
with that attitude of white supremacy.

00:58:31.900 --> 00:58:32.920
Is it a nightmare?

00:58:32.920 --> 00:58:34.180
Is it his sunlamp?

00:58:34.180 --> 00:58:35.980
Is it a medical condition?

00:58:35.980 --> 00:58:37.840
It turns out he's just black,

00:58:37.840 --> 00:58:42.120
having grown belatedly
into his family lineage.

00:58:42.120 --> 00:58:45.970
The black director of this
movie, Melvin Van Peebles,

00:58:45.970 --> 00:58:48.750
who just died yesterday,
I'm so sorry to say,

00:58:48.750 --> 00:58:53.260
insisted on two changes to the script.

00:58:53.260 --> 00:58:57.580
One, he cast a black
actor, Godfrey Cambridge,

00:58:57.580 --> 00:59:00.313
who is only briefly in
white phase at the start.

00:59:01.160 --> 00:59:04.623
Two, the race transformation
isn't reversed at the end.

00:59:05.660 --> 00:59:07.640
The screenwriter, Raucher,

00:59:07.640 --> 00:59:10.250
originally had had the white hero wake up

00:59:10.250 --> 00:59:12.490
and realize it was all a dream.

00:59:12.490 --> 00:59:16.300
But in Van Peebles' cut,
the man stays black.

00:59:16.300 --> 00:59:19.530
The denouement grows moody and somber.

00:59:19.530 --> 00:59:20.840
White neighbors threatened,

00:59:20.840 --> 00:59:24.160
then bribed the newly
black hero to leave town.

00:59:24.160 --> 00:59:26.770
His wife rejects his sexual advances.

00:59:26.770 --> 00:59:30.340
She says, I'm still liberal,
but only to a point.

00:59:30.340 --> 00:59:32.980
And she absconds with their kids.

00:59:32.980 --> 00:59:34.940
Eventually he starts
his own black business

00:59:34.940 --> 00:59:36.990
and joins the black community.

00:59:36.990 --> 00:59:40.060
The film ends with him
leading a group of black men

00:59:40.060 --> 00:59:43.330
in a seeming embrace of black power.

00:59:43.330 --> 00:59:45.280
The shift between the first

00:59:45.280 --> 00:59:47.160
and the last parts of "Watermelon Man"

00:59:47.160 --> 00:59:51.263
make the whole an uncanny,
avant-garde work of art.

00:59:52.260 --> 00:59:53.860
We see the same medley of tone

00:59:53.860 --> 00:59:57.380
in contemporary race transformation
tales by black artists.

00:59:57.380 --> 00:59:59.020
In Jordan Peele's "Get Out",

00:59:59.020 --> 01:00:01.100
white wealthy people buy black bodies

01:00:01.100 --> 01:00:02.920
to inhabit for physical perks.

01:00:02.920 --> 01:00:05.140
Stronger tendons, a longer life.

01:00:05.140 --> 01:00:08.860
And for one character, a
better photographic eye,

01:00:08.860 --> 01:00:11.320
implying that Du Bois's second-sight,

01:00:11.320 --> 01:00:12.863
has become a commodity.

01:00:13.960 --> 01:00:17.090
Identity transfer takes place
through the quasi-medical form

01:00:17.090 --> 01:00:18.610
of brain transplants:

01:00:18.610 --> 01:00:22.030
a coagula procedure,
again, invoking blood,

01:00:22.030 --> 01:00:23.900
that starts with hypnosis,

01:00:23.900 --> 01:00:25.900
a spoon circling the inside of a teacup,

01:00:25.900 --> 01:00:28.933
which you would see
Peele posing with here.

01:00:29.830 --> 01:00:32.340
This procedure then
moves to transplantation,

01:00:32.340 --> 01:00:34.240
a partial lobotomy and replacement

01:00:34.240 --> 01:00:37.000
with the brain of a white master.

01:00:37.000 --> 01:00:39.410
The film literalizes double consciousness

01:00:39.410 --> 01:00:41.200
through medicalized zombies,

01:00:41.200 --> 01:00:45.103
who are black and white,
slave and master in one body.

01:00:46.560 --> 01:00:47.960
In Maurice Carlos Ruffin's

01:00:47.960 --> 01:00:50.850
2019 burlesque "We Cast a Shadow",

01:00:50.850 --> 01:00:54.040
the racist condescension
of his white coworkers

01:00:54.040 --> 01:00:56.020
induce as a black New Orleans businessman

01:00:56.020 --> 01:00:57.920
to dress up like a Zulu chief

01:00:57.920 --> 01:01:00.453
and dance to a dark tribal beat.

01:01:01.530 --> 01:01:03.510
We learned that he is obsessed

01:01:03.510 --> 01:01:05.100
with earning enough copper coins

01:01:05.100 --> 01:01:07.580
to afford a new-fangled surgical procedure

01:01:07.580 --> 01:01:09.780
called demelanization.

01:01:09.780 --> 01:01:12.560
Not for himself, but
for his mixed race son

01:01:12.560 --> 01:01:14.840
whose dark birthmark threatens to spread

01:01:14.840 --> 01:01:16.423
over his entire face.

01:01:17.500 --> 01:01:21.240
I think the recent interest
in trans racial identity

01:01:21.240 --> 01:01:24.770
is the reason we get a very
funny documentary style skit

01:01:24.770 --> 01:01:27.550
on Donald Glover's TV show "Atlanta."

01:01:27.550 --> 01:01:30.680
We see an interview with a
black teenager with dark skin,

01:01:30.680 --> 01:01:33.443
who believes he's a 35-year-old white man.

01:01:34.970 --> 01:01:37.670
These works of race transformation

01:01:37.670 --> 01:01:41.740
use satire to highlight
the monstrosity of racism,

01:01:41.740 --> 01:01:44.870
especially the way that even
apparently liberal whiteness

01:01:44.870 --> 01:01:48.120
can abruptly tip into
paternalism and worse,

01:01:48.120 --> 01:01:49.870
horrific violence.

01:01:49.870 --> 01:01:51.270
Like white authors,

01:01:51.270 --> 01:01:53.480
the black authors of
race transformation tales

01:01:53.480 --> 01:01:57.950
play with these tropes and
elaborate unfamiliar themes.

01:01:57.950 --> 01:02:01.830
Sexual exoticism and disgust,
penis size comes up here too,

01:02:01.830 --> 01:02:04.710
or as Ruffin's narrator puts it, quote,

01:02:04.710 --> 01:02:07.783
the myth that all us black men
were hung like Clydesdales.

01:02:08.780 --> 01:02:10.780
We also see rifts on the suggestion

01:02:10.780 --> 01:02:13.310
that blackness is somehow
closer to the body,

01:02:13.310 --> 01:02:17.120
as well as the spiritualized
notion of a racial soul.

01:02:17.120 --> 01:02:19.210
But I also think these black author texts

01:02:19.210 --> 01:02:22.993
offer a counter model to the
idea that race is property.

01:02:24.530 --> 01:02:27.400
If we look at the three
features of this model,

01:02:27.400 --> 01:02:30.493
we can see how black
author texts undo them.

01:02:31.400 --> 01:02:33.690
For one thing, race in these stories

01:02:33.690 --> 01:02:37.250
is not a set of fixed bodily properties.

01:02:37.250 --> 01:02:40.640
When it comes to phenotype,
black communities in America

01:02:40.640 --> 01:02:43.030
have always been heterogeneous,

01:02:43.030 --> 01:02:46.603
multiple shades, shapes
and textures abound.

01:02:47.630 --> 01:02:50.230
These stories render skin or hair

01:02:50.230 --> 01:02:53.830
or features based conceptions
of race altogether absurd,

01:02:53.830 --> 01:02:56.150
precisely by showing up the fact

01:02:56.150 --> 01:02:59.270
that many white Americans
have black blood in the family

01:02:59.270 --> 01:03:00.323
and vice versa.

01:03:02.330 --> 01:03:05.520
And in historical terms,
the surreal one-drop rule

01:03:05.520 --> 01:03:09.230
meant that even an enslaved
person who could pass

01:03:09.230 --> 01:03:13.930
was barred from buying
whiteness and full freedom.

01:03:13.930 --> 01:03:16.110
In short, race was never a property

01:03:16.110 --> 01:03:18.333
that black people could buy or own.

01:03:19.270 --> 01:03:21.490
We might even say the
black American cultures

01:03:21.490 --> 01:03:24.590
came into being in resistance to property,

01:03:24.590 --> 01:03:28.510
as a refusal of the very
idea of purchasing people

01:03:28.510 --> 01:03:30.500
or their ways of speaking, making art

01:03:30.500 --> 01:03:32.070
and relating to each other.

01:03:32.070 --> 01:03:34.170
Blackness is, in the parlance of scholars

01:03:34.170 --> 01:03:36.890
like Fred Moten, fugitive.

01:03:36.890 --> 01:03:38.900
This is a matter of adaptation.

01:03:38.900 --> 01:03:40.120
For cultures to survive,

01:03:40.120 --> 01:03:42.470
they must be willing to grow and change.

01:03:42.470 --> 01:03:46.520
They cannot be pegged to
specific properties or values.

01:03:46.520 --> 01:03:48.377
This would constrict them or turn them

01:03:48.377 --> 01:03:51.363
into a parody of themselves.

01:03:52.830 --> 01:03:54.860
Indeed, when we take a Pan-African view

01:03:54.860 --> 01:03:58.260
of black diaspora communities,
we cannot help but notice

01:03:58.260 --> 01:04:00.930
how radically inclusive they are.

01:04:00.930 --> 01:04:04.570
Welcoming biracial and
multi-ethnic people,

01:04:04.570 --> 01:04:08.780
even those who had no choice
in the rate-induced attenuation

01:04:08.780 --> 01:04:10.770
of their blackness.

01:04:10.770 --> 01:04:13.920
Compare this to the fastidious
parsing and policing,

01:04:13.920 --> 01:04:17.030
even in the law, of who is white

01:04:17.030 --> 01:04:20.873
when it comes to American white politics.

01:04:22.170 --> 01:04:24.373
So in the black conception of race,

01:04:25.270 --> 01:04:29.750
blackness cannot belong to a
proper self, an individual.

01:04:29.750 --> 01:04:32.580
It is always relational and communal,

01:04:32.580 --> 01:04:34.053
because it is inclusive.

01:04:35.300 --> 01:04:38.100
What black authored race
transformation tales know

01:04:38.100 --> 01:04:42.690
is that personhood and race
are not fungible, but relative.

01:04:42.690 --> 01:04:45.720
If the white theory of
race as being property

01:04:45.720 --> 01:04:47.543
is emblematized by money,

01:04:49.410 --> 01:04:52.700
the black theory of race as
what escapes being property

01:04:52.700 --> 01:04:54.400
can be analogized to something else

01:04:54.400 --> 01:04:56.623
we engage with daily: time.

01:04:57.680 --> 01:04:59.990
Both analogies, money and time,

01:04:59.990 --> 01:05:03.880
convey how race is at
once real and unreal.

01:05:03.880 --> 01:05:08.880
We impose divisions and values
onto material cash and coins.

01:05:09.000 --> 01:05:11.220
We impose divisions and values

01:05:11.220 --> 01:05:13.433
onto the invisible passage of time.

01:05:14.280 --> 01:05:18.440
But a money metaphor for
race is flashy, fickle,

01:05:18.440 --> 01:05:21.010
fluctuating, a spinning coin of value,

01:05:21.010 --> 01:05:24.100
heads and tails
alternately coming to view.

01:05:24.100 --> 01:05:27.310
A time metaphor is steady, ongoing,

01:05:27.310 --> 01:05:28.963
and open to contingency.

01:05:30.280 --> 01:05:34.470
A history, an oscillation, a process.

01:05:34.470 --> 01:05:38.690
Again, I wanna emphasize,
race is not a genetic reality,

01:05:38.690 --> 01:05:41.120
but insofar as it is an identity

01:05:41.120 --> 01:05:43.860
or a culture with real-world consequences;

01:05:43.860 --> 01:05:46.690
it entails time and effort.

01:05:46.690 --> 01:05:49.120
Hence, the very different emphases

01:05:49.120 --> 01:05:52.980
on the diversity, duration and difficulty

01:05:52.980 --> 01:05:55.750
of race transformation in texts by authors

01:05:55.750 --> 01:05:57.710
of distinct races.

01:05:57.710 --> 01:06:02.710
So a story by a white author
like Keeler or Keller,

01:06:02.810 --> 01:06:06.340
a journalistic account
by Griffin or Sprigle

01:06:06.340 --> 01:06:09.430
can make the process of
becoming black seem easy,

01:06:09.430 --> 01:06:12.500
or rather facile, a
matter of darkened skin,

01:06:12.500 --> 01:06:15.803
minstrel-level dialect,
living in the hood, and so on.

01:06:17.220 --> 01:06:21.020
But Van Peebles' "Watermelon"
slows down considerably

01:06:21.020 --> 01:06:24.300
once the protagonist learns
that he is black for good.

01:06:24.300 --> 01:06:29.300
He spends time, he hangs
out, he learns, he grows.

01:06:29.320 --> 01:06:32.063
Over the years, he becomes conscious.

01:06:33.160 --> 01:06:35.840
In "Get Out", putting
a white person's mind

01:06:35.840 --> 01:06:38.440
inside a black person's
body is hardly sufficient

01:06:38.440 --> 01:06:40.250
for race transformation.

01:06:40.250 --> 01:06:41.910
Their body stay black,

01:06:41.910 --> 01:06:44.410
and sliver of their black
consciousness remains

01:06:44.410 --> 01:06:48.670
emerging from the sunken place
in brief emotional glimpses.

01:06:48.670 --> 01:06:53.670
Tears, anger, and abrupt
warnings for the hero to get out.

01:06:53.990 --> 01:06:57.010
We come to understand that
blackness isn't reducible

01:06:57.010 --> 01:06:59.500
to the body, but is rather something

01:06:59.500 --> 01:07:02.600
that comes from experience over time.

01:07:02.600 --> 01:07:04.690
Something that lasts.

01:07:04.690 --> 01:07:07.010
This is conveyed in the film
through the black hero's

01:07:07.010 --> 01:07:08.860
literally salvific relationship

01:07:08.860 --> 01:07:11.173
with his black best friend, Rod.

01:07:12.830 --> 01:07:16.130
In some, speculative race transformation

01:07:16.130 --> 01:07:20.270
tries to short circuit the
time of racial passing,

01:07:20.270 --> 01:07:24.100
to take a shortcut to the
pleasure, pain, passion,

01:07:24.100 --> 01:07:26.500
and perversity of race.

01:07:26.500 --> 01:07:29.080
In so doing, it shortchanges the way

01:07:29.080 --> 01:07:33.140
race can be an experience
of culture and community,

01:07:33.140 --> 01:07:37.250
one that can only happen
in time, over time

01:07:37.250 --> 01:07:38.343
and through time.

01:07:39.920 --> 01:07:43.370
It is an experience that we
would want not to get out from,

01:07:43.370 --> 01:07:47.170
but to move deeper into,
move gradually with,

01:07:47.170 --> 01:07:52.170
rather than passing, rather
than reverse passing,

01:07:52.410 --> 01:07:55.120
rather than switching between races

01:07:55.120 --> 01:07:58.930
and rather indulge in
Barnum's taxonomic barking.

01:07:58.930 --> 01:08:00.500
Perhaps we might treat race

01:08:00.500 --> 01:08:02.890
as what I hope this talk has provoked,

01:08:02.890 --> 01:08:07.890
a contingent, curious and
ongoing movement of the mind.

01:08:08.570 --> 01:08:09.923
Thank you so much.

01:08:11.150 --> 01:08:12.690
- Thank you very much, Namwali.

01:08:12.690 --> 01:08:15.540
This is the place where there
would be thunderous applause

01:08:15.540 --> 01:08:19.540
if we weren't in this Zoom strange space.

01:08:19.540 --> 01:08:22.763
Thank you for taking us
through a truly bizarre corpus,

01:08:23.720 --> 01:08:28.100
with unfortunately familiar
horrors lurking behind it.

01:08:28.100 --> 01:08:29.520
So I appreciate that.

01:08:29.520 --> 01:08:32.120
I want to encourage audience members

01:08:32.120 --> 01:08:34.127
to put questions into the Q&amp;A.

01:08:35.900 --> 01:08:36.970
Anything you want to ask,

01:08:36.970 --> 01:08:39.490
we'll try to communicate
that to our speaker.

01:08:39.490 --> 01:08:42.300
And I want to introduce briefly Tina Post,

01:08:42.300 --> 01:08:43.450
who's assistant professor

01:08:43.450 --> 01:08:46.090
in the Department of English
Language and Literature.

01:08:46.090 --> 01:08:48.850
She's on the Committee on
Theater and Performance Studies

01:08:48.850 --> 01:08:51.000
in the Center for the Study
of Race, Politics and Culture

01:08:51.000 --> 01:08:53.070
at the University of Chicago,

01:08:53.070 --> 01:08:56.710
Chicago PhD here at Yale
and performance studies,

01:08:56.710 --> 01:09:00.240
and we do miss her.

01:09:00.240 --> 01:09:02.010
I want to point you to a monograph

01:09:02.010 --> 01:09:06.350
that should be out very soon,
within the next year or two,

01:09:06.350 --> 01:09:10.180
called Deadpan: The Aesthetics
of Black Inexpression.

01:09:10.180 --> 01:09:12.960
And welcome Tina, thanks
for helping us out.

01:09:12.960 --> 01:09:13.793
- Thank you.

01:09:15.450 --> 01:09:19.960
- So we can run this as we all want to.

01:09:19.960 --> 01:09:21.743
I could start with one question.

01:09:22.720 --> 01:09:23.820
I have a lot of questions,

01:09:23.820 --> 01:09:26.540
and I'm really happy
you came through Du Bois

01:09:26.540 --> 01:09:29.780
and double consciousness because it seemed

01:09:31.100 --> 01:09:34.390
what's left out in all of these accounts

01:09:34.390 --> 01:09:38.760
is anything like the
intellectual or consciousness

01:09:38.760 --> 01:09:42.430
of black experience.

01:09:42.430 --> 01:09:45.170
It's as though it reminded
me of all those pictures

01:09:45.170 --> 01:09:47.440
of Einstein or Einstein wigs,

01:09:47.440 --> 01:09:50.360
as though you could put
that on and that was it.

01:09:50.360 --> 01:09:55.120
But there's a whole mental experience

01:09:55.120 --> 01:09:56.120
that goes along with it.

01:09:56.120 --> 01:10:00.573
Does anyone make reference
to wisdom, consciousness,

01:10:02.300 --> 01:10:04.880
intelligence as something
you would pick up

01:10:04.880 --> 01:10:06.423
going over to the other side?

01:10:07.310 --> 01:10:10.230
- Not going over to the black side, no.

01:10:10.230 --> 01:10:13.940
I mean, I think Du Bois,
in this short story

01:10:13.940 --> 01:10:15.970
which remains unpublished,

01:10:15.970 --> 01:10:19.420
he referred to it as unprophetable,

01:10:19.420 --> 01:10:23.550
with a misspelling of
prophet, as in the prophetic,

01:10:23.550 --> 01:10:28.550
which I think is quite a
funny accident to make.

01:10:28.770 --> 01:10:30.880
But he's the only one who says

01:10:30.880 --> 01:10:33.190
that the white Harvard undergraduate

01:10:33.190 --> 01:10:37.000
who's been transformed into a black person

01:10:37.890 --> 01:10:40.950
is going to have access
to the fourth dimension,

01:10:40.950 --> 01:10:45.430
which, in the kind of
general parlance of the time,

01:10:45.430 --> 01:10:47.430
would've meant time.

01:10:47.430 --> 01:10:51.250
So I find that quite interesting.

01:10:51.250 --> 01:10:53.860
And actually Robert Gilbert Wells,

01:10:53.860 --> 01:10:58.860
when he transforms his master,
or ex master, Mr. Jones

01:10:59.040 --> 01:11:02.540
into a black man, his wife is like,

01:11:02.540 --> 01:11:05.800
ah, you gotta change him
back, he can't handle it.

01:11:05.800 --> 01:11:06.810
He's not like us.

01:11:06.810 --> 01:11:09.400
He hasn't suffered to be black.

01:11:09.400 --> 01:11:10.960
He doesn't know how to deal with this.

01:11:10.960 --> 01:11:15.890
And he says, if he were more,

01:11:15.890 --> 01:11:19.230
if he were stronger,
having access to blackness

01:11:19.230 --> 01:11:21.570
wouldn't just be a way
of accessing suffering,

01:11:21.570 --> 01:11:25.130
but would actually be a way of
expanding his consciousness.

01:11:25.130 --> 01:11:27.210
They're engaged throughout this text

01:11:27.210 --> 01:11:30.790
in a kind of Socratic
debate back and forth

01:11:30.790 --> 01:11:35.790
about intellectual and social
problems facing the country.

01:11:38.720 --> 01:11:41.860
But this transformation has meant to be

01:11:41.860 --> 01:11:44.603
both a kind of social experiment.

01:11:45.860 --> 01:11:50.100
Mr. Jones learns that his
own brother is probably black

01:11:50.100 --> 01:11:54.560
and they reunite as a result
of this transformation,

01:11:54.560 --> 01:11:57.410
but he also, Sam really thinks,

01:11:57.410 --> 01:11:59.920
if he becomes black, he'll have access

01:11:59.920 --> 01:12:01.570
to wisdom and knowledge.

01:12:01.570 --> 01:12:04.930
But again, it's striking to me

01:12:04.930 --> 01:12:07.960
that it's the black
authors who perceive this,

01:12:07.960 --> 01:12:10.400
who perceive that becoming
black would not just be

01:12:10.400 --> 01:12:11.953
about learning to dance better.

01:12:14.240 --> 01:12:16.023
- Tina, I give the word over to you.

01:12:18.510 --> 01:12:20.600
- I don't know that I have
too much to add to that,

01:12:20.600 --> 01:12:24.030
except that it's remarkable that the one,

01:12:24.030 --> 01:12:25.780
maybe the one thing that's so consistent

01:12:25.780 --> 01:12:30.110
between the narrative
authored by white authors

01:12:30.110 --> 01:12:33.280
than those of black is the
way in which they acknowledge

01:12:33.280 --> 01:12:37.810
that blackness is this
unknown country, right?

01:12:37.810 --> 01:12:41.050
And I think one of the things that is,

01:12:41.050 --> 01:12:44.640
maybe implicitly remarked
upon in these narratives

01:12:44.640 --> 01:12:47.780
is that, historically, black
people have always known

01:12:47.780 --> 01:12:49.720
white people very, very well.

01:12:49.720 --> 01:12:52.120
We've raised their children,
wash their clothes,

01:12:52.120 --> 01:12:54.800
cook their meals, we know whiteness

01:12:54.800 --> 01:12:58.390
in a way that whiteness has
never known blackness in return.

01:12:58.390 --> 01:13:01.700
And both, the white and black
authors, it seemed to me,

01:13:01.700 --> 01:13:03.333
did sort of allude to the,

01:13:04.620 --> 01:13:08.240
I'm entering this unknown
territory, and truly it was.

01:13:08.240 --> 01:13:12.617
So yeah, I guess that's
maybe the one thing I'd add.

01:13:12.617 --> 01:13:14.750
- That's a great observation.

01:13:14.750 --> 01:13:16.730
I think one of the ways we can see it

01:13:16.730 --> 01:13:20.400
is if you compare these
texts to passing literature,

01:13:20.400 --> 01:13:23.520
where there's not very much

01:13:25.910 --> 01:13:30.060
by way of study or effort when you pass,

01:13:30.060 --> 01:13:32.590
it's a choice that can
happen kind of seamlessly

01:13:32.590 --> 01:13:35.300
because you know how to
live as a white person,

01:13:35.300 --> 01:13:37.003
you just have more privileges.

01:13:38.290 --> 01:13:39.930
And you can don't have
to read certain things,

01:13:39.930 --> 01:13:42.810
you don't have to, whereas in these texts,

01:13:42.810 --> 01:13:46.310
when you become black, there's
all of this tension about,

01:13:46.310 --> 01:13:48.790
will I know how to dance

01:13:48.790 --> 01:13:51.296
and will I know how to play basketball

01:13:51.296 --> 01:13:55.900
and automatically give me this knowledge

01:13:55.900 --> 01:13:59.460
or this body knowledge, and
there's all this kind of effort.

01:13:59.460 --> 01:14:03.080
That's the way the magic and
the science fictional gimmick

01:14:03.080 --> 01:14:04.990
is necessary in some ways,

01:14:04.990 --> 01:14:06.700
because it can't just happen naturally

01:14:06.700 --> 01:14:08.300
the way that it does in passing.

01:14:13.320 --> 01:14:14.620
- Thanks, that's great.

01:14:14.620 --> 01:14:16.980
Tina, I don't know if you
wanna open up any topics

01:14:16.980 --> 01:14:18.053
at this point.

01:14:20.140 --> 01:14:21.420
- I mean, I'll say a couple things

01:14:21.420 --> 01:14:23.463
that seem interesting to me.

01:14:25.153 --> 01:14:26.910
The whole top is wonderful, first of all.

01:14:26.910 --> 01:14:27.743
Thank you so much.

01:14:27.743 --> 01:14:31.593
It was fascinating and totally fruitful.

01:14:32.780 --> 01:14:35.410
There were a couple of
thoughts that I had that,

01:14:35.410 --> 01:14:38.040
just things that came up
to me as I was reading it

01:14:38.040 --> 01:14:39.670
that maybe you just put on the floor.

01:14:39.670 --> 01:14:41.260
And, of course, I have no way of knowing

01:14:41.260 --> 01:14:46.260
if you're as familiar with
these, some of these ideas.

01:14:46.370 --> 01:14:51.250
One is this bonkers
one-off movie from the '90s

01:14:51.250 --> 01:14:53.710
that's maybe this historical juncture

01:14:53.710 --> 01:14:56.590
that we may have gone down
but didn't called "Suture."

01:14:56.590 --> 01:14:59.967
- Oh yes, I love that.
- Yeah, okay.

01:14:59.967 --> 01:15:01.450
So the premise of this movie,

01:15:01.450 --> 01:15:03.840
which I saw many years
ago and barely remember,

01:15:03.840 --> 01:15:08.840
but it's a black and white
film involving twin brothers,

01:15:10.810 --> 01:15:13.440
one of whom is white and
one of whom is black.

01:15:13.440 --> 01:15:16.310
And not like passing black, just black.

01:15:16.310 --> 01:15:19.280
And absolutely nothing is said about it.

01:15:19.280 --> 01:15:22.330
And so there's no
acknowledgment that they're not,

01:15:22.330 --> 01:15:26.300
that they don't look alike
or constant in any way.

01:15:26.300 --> 01:15:30.860
So that was sort of one thing
I wanted to toss in this,

01:15:30.860 --> 01:15:32.260
perhaps fruitful.

01:15:32.260 --> 01:15:35.753
And then, what was it,
there was one other thing.

01:15:38.150 --> 01:15:41.180
Oh, I'll save the other thing
for later if it comes up.

01:15:41.180 --> 01:15:42.666
It's, I think, less important.

01:15:42.666 --> 01:15:45.630
But yeah, you nodded as
though you've seen "Suture."

01:15:45.630 --> 01:15:48.370
- Yeah, I've seen it and
I screened it actually

01:15:48.370 --> 01:15:53.370
at a conference that I organized
at UC Berkeley on Faces,

01:15:53.815 --> 01:15:58.815
because I was very interested
in all of these close up shots

01:16:00.010 --> 01:16:01.070
of the face.

01:16:01.070 --> 01:16:03.410
'Cause it's obviously also kind of troping

01:16:03.410 --> 01:16:06.710
on H. G. Wells's "Invisible Man",

01:16:06.710 --> 01:16:09.014
because you have this face that's,

01:16:09.014 --> 01:16:12.230
a face is damaged and is
wrapped up in all these bandages

01:16:12.230 --> 01:16:14.290
and it's slowly unpeeled and then,

01:16:14.290 --> 01:16:18.260
like when the suturing
of the skin is happening

01:16:18.260 --> 01:16:21.840
to the black face, there's all these very

01:16:21.840 --> 01:16:24.310
kind of intense close-ups of that face.

01:16:24.310 --> 01:16:25.340
And so I was very interested

01:16:25.340 --> 01:16:28.850
in the extent to which
the face is the locus

01:16:28.850 --> 01:16:31.300
of racial identity for us.

01:16:31.300 --> 01:16:35.370
But also in the way that the
transformation of the face

01:16:35.370 --> 01:16:39.120
is something that's presented
as an aesthetic project.

01:16:39.120 --> 01:16:42.560
And so in my book, on faces
called "Stranger Faces,"

01:16:42.560 --> 01:16:44.170
which was published last year,

01:16:44.170 --> 01:16:47.060
I talked about this and
a little bit in relation

01:16:47.060 --> 01:16:50.010
to Michael Jackson's
surgeries on his face as well.

01:16:50.010 --> 01:16:52.780
So I was interested in that sense.

01:16:52.780 --> 01:16:55.874
I mean, that movie is
interesting because it,

01:16:55.874 --> 01:16:59.620
the scientific or the
science fictional trope

01:16:59.620 --> 01:17:03.820
of race transformation isn't
happening in the diegesis,

01:17:03.820 --> 01:17:06.020
in the narrative; it's
happening, as you say,

01:17:06.020 --> 01:17:09.220
in the viewer, who's given
no clue as to why this is,

01:17:09.220 --> 01:17:12.030
and so you're just constantly working

01:17:12.030 --> 01:17:14.970
against your own assumptions
of what race looks like

01:17:14.970 --> 01:17:16.070
as you watch the film.

01:17:20.320 --> 01:17:21.153
- That's terrific.

01:17:21.153 --> 01:17:24.203
Maybe I'll throw in a paranoid question.

01:17:26.224 --> 01:17:28.853
And you were talking especially about,

01:17:30.860 --> 01:17:33.150
those people who did kind
of ethnographic study

01:17:33.150 --> 01:17:37.520
and did the black face and
went and wrote these books,

01:17:37.520 --> 01:17:41.200
I couldn't help but think
of things like COINTELPRO

01:17:41.200 --> 01:17:44.450
and other programs in which
there was infiltration

01:17:44.450 --> 01:17:45.780
and I wondered whether there was,

01:17:45.780 --> 01:17:49.283
if you thought there
was a kind of paranoia,

01:17:51.180 --> 01:17:56.103
fears of uprisings and
whether these were spies.

01:17:56.946 --> 01:17:58.843
- Oh, I wouldn't know.

01:17:58.843 --> 01:18:02.437
I mean, Alisha Gaines's
book I relied on heavily,

01:18:02.437 --> 01:18:05.290
"Black For a Day", and
it's a brilliant analysis.

01:18:05.290 --> 01:18:08.590
And she went into the archives
of these three writers

01:18:08.590 --> 01:18:13.113
and didn't seem to uncover
anything of that nature.

01:18:14.650 --> 01:18:16.080
But what's interesting about it

01:18:16.080 --> 01:18:20.800
is the very desire to
publish a memoir about it

01:18:20.800 --> 01:18:23.873
and this idea that this
is kind of confessional,

01:18:24.840 --> 01:18:26.880
it's filled with all these emotions

01:18:26.880 --> 01:18:29.980
that have to do with shame
and prurience and taboo

01:18:29.980 --> 01:18:34.120
suggests to me that they
definitely weren't spies

01:18:34.120 --> 01:18:37.150
because their whole point
was exposing (mumbles)

01:18:37.150 --> 01:18:39.163
and kind of playing off of it.

01:18:40.850 --> 01:18:41.683
It's a very--

01:18:41.683 --> 01:18:45.550
- That's even if they're not
good spies for the government,

01:18:45.550 --> 01:18:48.270
there's something like a
race spy reporting back,

01:18:48.270 --> 01:18:50.250
in the way that Tina mentioned,

01:18:50.250 --> 01:18:52.810
to assure the white population

01:18:52.810 --> 01:18:54.910
that the uprising is not gonna happen now.

01:18:56.015 --> 01:18:57.740
- That's true, but what's interesting

01:18:57.740 --> 01:19:02.740
is that at least Griffin and Halsell

01:19:03.280 --> 01:19:07.630
published parts of their work
in black-oriented magazines,

01:19:07.630 --> 01:19:09.150
like Ebony and Sepia.

01:19:09.150 --> 01:19:12.370
So the audience was not
just the white community,

01:19:12.370 --> 01:19:14.450
it was also the black community,

01:19:14.450 --> 01:19:16.950
which feels to me like a kind of attempt

01:19:16.950 --> 01:19:19.860
at atonement, right?

01:19:19.860 --> 01:19:22.230
You have to know that I tried

01:19:22.230 --> 01:19:25.530
and that it's no use to
tell my white liberal,

01:19:25.530 --> 01:19:27.433
progressive friends that I tried.

01:19:28.820 --> 01:19:31.525
It's I need to tell the black community

01:19:31.525 --> 01:19:34.030
how deeply I went into this

01:19:34.030 --> 01:19:37.140
and how I have suffered
what they have suffered.

01:19:37.140 --> 01:19:38.493
So yeah, it's interesting.

01:19:39.410 --> 01:19:40.960
They're double agents, I guess.

01:19:41.910 --> 01:19:45.067
- Okay, that does bring me sort
of around to the other thing

01:19:45.067 --> 01:19:47.673
that I was thinking of
mentioning and then I, yeah.

01:19:49.940 --> 01:19:53.110
Jackie Sibblies Drury's
"Fairview", the play.

01:19:53.110 --> 01:19:54.690
I don't know if you know it.

01:19:54.690 --> 01:19:57.173
Okay, so it begins with
a conversation about,

01:19:58.030 --> 01:19:59.490
no, just begin, but--
- Part of it.

01:19:59.490 --> 01:20:00.390
Okay, yes, yes.

01:20:00.390 --> 01:20:01.990
- There's a conversation about what,

01:20:01.990 --> 01:20:04.380
if you could change your race,
who would you be and why?

01:20:04.380 --> 01:20:08.013
That happens as this overarching narrative

01:20:10.600 --> 01:20:12.040
over what's happening on the stage.

01:20:12.040 --> 01:20:15.590
And then there's sort of
this everything comes apart.

01:20:15.590 --> 01:20:20.590
And then there's a moment of
meant to be a black communion

01:20:21.970 --> 01:20:24.040
and community, which
seems very much in keeping

01:20:24.040 --> 01:20:27.760
with your sense of racial relation.

01:20:27.760 --> 01:20:30.661
And yeah, it's fun, this tall,

01:20:30.661 --> 01:20:35.661
what might be considered a sort
of confessional theater view

01:20:37.230 --> 01:20:41.880
as like, from all the white,
usually white theater goers,

01:20:41.880 --> 01:20:46.200
who go and review plays for newspapers.

01:20:46.200 --> 01:20:51.200
And I think it was a fruitful

01:20:51.660 --> 01:20:54.250
and fraught experience for many and,

01:20:54.250 --> 01:20:56.963
but it definitely needed
to be sort of unpacked.

01:20:58.078 --> 01:20:59.850
And I love teaching that play because yes,

01:20:59.850 --> 01:21:01.573
there's a lot to unpack there.

01:21:02.480 --> 01:21:04.710
But I've realized if you haven't seen it,

01:21:04.710 --> 01:21:07.470
this is perhaps unfair,
but maybe you experienced

01:21:07.470 --> 01:21:10.773
some of those kinds of
reviews when it was big.

01:21:12.005 --> 01:21:14.970
- I didn't read the
reviews, but I do know,

01:21:14.970 --> 01:21:16.360
I do know the play.

01:21:16.360 --> 01:21:20.980
And there is a history, and
I cut this from the talk,

01:21:20.980 --> 01:21:23.580
but in the essay I talk about,

01:21:23.580 --> 01:21:25.320
the musical "Finian's Rainbow",

01:21:25.320 --> 01:21:27.320
which was also turned into a film

01:21:28.990 --> 01:21:31.080
directed by Francis Ford Coppola

01:21:31.080 --> 01:21:32.830
in which there is a scene,

01:21:32.830 --> 01:21:37.573
in which a racist white senator is cursed,

01:21:38.995 --> 01:21:41.660
that's turned into a black person again

01:21:41.660 --> 01:21:45.620
in order to force him to change his ways.

01:21:45.620 --> 01:21:48.640
And so I think the
history within the theater

01:21:48.640 --> 01:21:51.253
and within film is really interesting.

01:21:53.060 --> 01:21:55.884
I didn't quite follow what the,

01:21:55.884 --> 01:22:00.797
what is the transformation plot
that happens in "Fairview"?

01:22:01.946 --> 01:22:04.910
- It's funny because it
isn't even actually a plot.

01:22:04.910 --> 01:22:07.640
So the plot of the play as such,

01:22:07.640 --> 01:22:09.650
there's sort of didactic layers to it,

01:22:09.650 --> 01:22:11.080
but the plot of the play as such

01:22:11.080 --> 01:22:15.920
is sort of a black family drama,

01:22:15.920 --> 01:22:17.970
or at least it starts that way.

01:22:17.970 --> 01:22:22.050
And as the over, the narrative,

01:22:22.050 --> 01:22:24.730
the voices come on sort of discussing,

01:22:24.730 --> 01:22:26.810
well, if you could change
your race, what would it be?

01:22:26.810 --> 01:22:30.380
And then, of course, the
answers are stereotypical.

01:22:30.380 --> 01:22:33.700
And also, there's someone
pushing against stereotypes

01:22:33.700 --> 01:22:38.450
and as, but the action on the stage

01:22:38.450 --> 01:22:40.520
becomes more and more stereotypical

01:22:40.520 --> 01:22:45.520
as that extra layer of sound
is transpiring on top of it.

01:22:46.950 --> 01:22:50.470
I hope that's a fair summary.

01:22:50.470 --> 01:22:53.340
I haven't re-read it in
a minute, but that's,

01:22:53.340 --> 01:22:55.162
I think that's a fair--

01:22:55.162 --> 01:22:58.973
- (audio warps) I think that
something that I've noticed in,

01:23:01.840 --> 01:23:04.500
one way of putting it is that slide I have

01:23:04.500 --> 01:23:05.720
or that note that I have,

01:23:05.720 --> 01:23:08.930
that I couldn't find any black
person being turned white

01:23:08.930 --> 01:23:10.133
as punishment.

01:23:14.490 --> 01:23:16.370
Whiteness is aspirational.

01:23:16.370 --> 01:23:19.410
And the assumption that
whiteness is aspirational

01:23:19.410 --> 01:23:22.620
is something that pervades
all of these texts.

01:23:22.620 --> 01:23:27.620
But in the white authored
texts, it's not troubled.

01:23:28.520 --> 01:23:30.330
It's just sort of seen as obvious

01:23:30.330 --> 01:23:32.130
that it would be better to be white.

01:23:33.110 --> 01:23:37.357
And this is why you have
this kind of hysteria,

01:23:38.490 --> 01:23:41.340
the idea that white people
could be turned black

01:23:41.340 --> 01:23:42.863
against their will, right?

01:23:44.160 --> 01:23:46.520
But in the black authored texts,

01:23:46.520 --> 01:23:48.839
there's very much a kind of sense that

01:23:48.839 --> 01:23:53.420
to be made white is not
necessarily what you want

01:23:53.420 --> 01:23:54.910
and that there's a cost.

01:23:54.910 --> 01:23:57.900
And this is why we have this
history in passing literature

01:23:57.900 --> 01:24:01.030
of it being presented as a kind of death.

01:24:01.030 --> 01:24:04.220
And it's also something
that really fascinates me

01:24:04.220 --> 01:24:06.380
just at a kind of personal level

01:24:06.380 --> 01:24:09.900
that whenever I'm asked about the fact

01:24:09.900 --> 01:24:13.360
that I have a white
parent, there's a kind of,

01:24:13.360 --> 01:24:15.830
sometimes there's the illusion
that I could be white,

01:24:15.830 --> 01:24:19.010
that I could pass, which I
don't think I've ever passed,

01:24:19.010 --> 01:24:21.060
but also that I would want to.

01:24:21.060 --> 01:24:23.450
And there's always a surprise when I say,

01:24:23.450 --> 01:24:25.610
I would never want to be white actually.

01:24:25.610 --> 01:24:29.360
And so I think there's something
along those lines, I think,

01:24:29.360 --> 01:24:32.020
maybe that is troubling to the reviewers,

01:24:32.020 --> 01:24:34.300
is actually even having the conversation

01:24:34.300 --> 01:24:37.640
of whether you would want or
not want to change your race

01:24:37.640 --> 01:24:40.240
produces a kind of tension,

01:24:40.240 --> 01:24:41.830
because there are these assumptions

01:24:41.830 --> 01:24:43.730
that these different people work with.

01:24:46.870 --> 01:24:47.703
- That's great.

01:24:47.703 --> 01:24:50.640
I wanna give just a
little time to the Q&amp;A.

01:24:50.640 --> 01:24:53.530
And I'm gonna read you a couple
of questions, is that okay?

01:24:53.530 --> 01:24:55.610
So one says, thank you
for the wonderful talk.

01:24:55.610 --> 01:24:57.850
I was wondering what
you make of the satire

01:24:57.850 --> 01:25:00.950
in "Black No More" directed
against black activists

01:25:00.950 --> 01:25:04.340
and intellectuals like W.E.B. Du Bois.

01:25:04.340 --> 01:25:05.173
- Yeah.

01:25:05.173 --> 01:25:10.070
I mean, Schuyler is a
great figure in some ways.

01:25:10.070 --> 01:25:15.070
I recommend the biography of
him written by Jeff Ferguson.

01:25:16.070 --> 01:25:18.350
He's a conservative in certain ways,

01:25:18.350 --> 01:25:22.200
and I think the leftist
politics of that time

01:25:22.200 --> 01:25:23.660
kind of bothered him.

01:25:23.660 --> 01:25:26.970
But I also think there's
an attempt to skewer

01:25:26.970 --> 01:25:31.220
the pretensions of that,
of the Harlem Renaissance,

01:25:31.220 --> 01:25:33.050
of that community at the time.

01:25:33.050 --> 01:25:36.080
And of course, one of the
things he makes a lot of

01:25:37.520 --> 01:25:39.230
that's a grist for his satirical mill

01:25:39.230 --> 01:25:42.370
is that Du Bois and other
people in the Harlem Renaissance

01:25:42.370 --> 01:25:43.940
were all very light-skinned.

01:25:43.940 --> 01:25:46.570
Du Bois himself could have passed.

01:25:46.570 --> 01:25:49.270
So there's a kind of a sense, I think,

01:25:49.270 --> 01:25:51.250
that he also wants to show up the colorism

01:25:51.250 --> 01:25:54.253
that's implicit in both the
black and the white community.

01:25:55.340 --> 01:25:58.840
He's satirizing power
dynamics and hierarchies

01:25:58.840 --> 01:26:02.390
that are within both of those communities.

01:26:02.390 --> 01:26:07.070
I mean, it's a pretty,
I think, short analysis

01:26:07.070 --> 01:26:08.740
of a really, really fascinating text,

01:26:08.740 --> 01:26:10.690
but that's what I would say about that.

01:26:11.670 --> 01:26:12.840
- Okay, here's another one.

01:26:12.840 --> 01:26:14.450
Thanks for a thought-provoking talk.

01:26:14.450 --> 01:26:17.340
I totally agree that race, in quotes,

01:26:17.340 --> 01:26:19.223
is experiences through time.

01:26:20.140 --> 01:26:21.821
So you have one believer.

01:26:21.821 --> 01:26:22.940
(laughs)

01:26:22.940 --> 01:26:24.850
I wonder how you would read a scenario

01:26:24.850 --> 01:26:26.780
of a white presenting person

01:26:26.780 --> 01:26:29.000
being embedded in the community of color

01:26:29.000 --> 01:26:31.373
and sticking a claim to
the identity of color.

01:26:32.380 --> 01:26:34.870
Would that be a legit identity performance

01:26:34.870 --> 01:26:35.703
in that connection?

01:26:35.703 --> 01:26:38.967
Are you familiar with David
Henry Hwang's satire play

01:26:38.967 --> 01:26:41.367
"Yellow Face" that was
made into a YouTube film?

01:26:43.330 --> 01:26:44.990
- I am not familiar with it.

01:26:44.990 --> 01:26:49.990
But it's interesting
because this sort of came up

01:26:51.000 --> 01:26:52.740
in a conversation I had yesterday

01:26:52.740 --> 01:26:56.820
when the trailer for the new
film of "Passing" came out.

01:26:56.820 --> 01:26:59.210
There's been a lot of conversation

01:26:59.210 --> 01:27:03.780
about whether the two
actresses cast in the film

01:27:03.780 --> 01:27:06.340
could themselves pass as white.

01:27:06.340 --> 01:27:09.640
And there's a lot, I mean,
one of the kind of tropes

01:27:09.640 --> 01:27:13.250
of passing for the black community is that

01:27:15.340 --> 01:27:16.960
if you see someone who's passing,

01:27:16.960 --> 01:27:20.660
you've got their number, you
could identify them as black.

01:27:20.660 --> 01:27:23.170
And so there are many people who say,

01:27:23.170 --> 01:27:27.370
well, the idea that one of
these or both of these women

01:27:27.370 --> 01:27:31.420
could pass as white means
that they could pass as white

01:27:31.420 --> 01:27:33.860
to other white people, right?

01:27:33.860 --> 01:27:36.340
But the reason this came up is because

01:27:36.340 --> 01:27:40.040
Rebecca Hall, the director
of the film, is herself,

01:27:40.040 --> 01:27:42.410
herself has black in the family, right?

01:27:42.410 --> 01:27:45.093
It turns out that her
grandfather was black.

01:27:46.130 --> 01:27:48.460
But no one in the family had registered

01:27:48.460 --> 01:27:51.030
or identified as black for her whole life.

01:27:51.030 --> 01:27:55.530
So she grew up as a white
passing black person

01:27:55.530 --> 01:27:58.070
but with no sense of being black.

01:27:58.070 --> 01:27:59.007
And she's an actress herself.

01:27:59.007 --> 01:28:03.060
And so I wondered why she
didn't star in the film.

01:28:03.060 --> 01:28:04.580
And I think even though she, I think,

01:28:04.580 --> 01:28:06.600
would've been a better casting,

01:28:06.600 --> 01:28:08.770
because she looks like she could be white

01:28:08.770 --> 01:28:11.460
and so you might believe
it, she wouldn't be as good

01:28:11.460 --> 01:28:15.210
because she doesn't have the
experience of being black,

01:28:15.210 --> 01:28:18.340
which is again this kind
of cultural experience.

01:28:18.340 --> 01:28:21.330
So I don't know, I don't
think a white person

01:28:21.330 --> 01:28:22.740
living in a black community

01:28:22.740 --> 01:28:24.420
or a person who's white identifying

01:28:24.420 --> 01:28:28.420
or who has no blackness in their family

01:28:28.420 --> 01:28:29.530
or in their community

01:28:29.530 --> 01:28:32.780
and who just embeds in a black community

01:28:32.780 --> 01:28:36.030
could be said to have
experience blackness, no.

01:28:36.030 --> 01:28:38.240
But they are having a different experience

01:28:38.240 --> 01:28:40.340
than a lot of white people, for sure.

01:28:40.340 --> 01:28:42.350
But you have to understand
that what you describe

01:28:42.350 --> 01:28:45.980
in this question is very
common, it's the South.

01:28:45.980 --> 01:28:46.873
I mean, there are a lot of,

01:28:46.873 --> 01:28:49.960
there's a lot of co-presence
of black and white people

01:28:49.960 --> 01:28:52.220
in the South but it's still
an incredibly segregated

01:28:52.220 --> 01:28:54.860
and racially divided community.

01:28:54.860 --> 01:28:59.190
So I don't think that experience is simply

01:28:59.190 --> 01:29:03.053
about being present
within a black community.

01:29:05.775 --> 01:29:07.790
And I think that staking that identity

01:29:07.790 --> 01:29:12.790
is very much akin to the kinds
of appropriative gestures

01:29:13.330 --> 01:29:17.240
that I was talking about
in some of these texts,

01:29:17.240 --> 01:29:19.650
where the desire to belong,

01:29:19.650 --> 01:29:21.370
the sense, oh, it's just allophilia,

01:29:21.370 --> 01:29:23.520
I just prefer black people and so on,

01:29:23.520 --> 01:29:26.320
that's fine and good if you
wanna live your life that way.

01:29:26.320 --> 01:29:28.680
But you can't then say,
I am there for black,

01:29:28.680 --> 01:29:30.580
that's not how it works unfortunately.

01:29:31.874 --> 01:29:33.870
- That is a beautiful
line on which to end.

01:29:33.870 --> 01:29:35.850
Okay, Tina, I'll give you (audio warps).

01:29:35.850 --> 01:29:38.600
- I was just gonna quickly
add just a couple of things.

01:29:39.670 --> 01:29:41.700
I agree with everything.

01:29:41.700 --> 01:29:45.870
But I would say maybe
two additional things.

01:29:45.870 --> 01:29:50.870
One is that, just very
much, as in the talk,

01:29:50.870 --> 01:29:55.187
I think one experience of racialization

01:29:56.261 --> 01:30:01.261
is exactly the sense of not being able

01:30:01.520 --> 01:30:03.120
to leave when you want to.

01:30:03.120 --> 01:30:05.887
And that's one of the things
that distinguishes the,

01:30:08.700 --> 01:30:13.700
being, I forget, a
white identifying person

01:30:14.630 --> 01:30:16.520
who is living a black life,

01:30:16.520 --> 01:30:20.240
is that, as in those fictional stories,

01:30:20.240 --> 01:30:24.050
when that person wants to stop, they can.

01:30:24.050 --> 01:30:27.030
And that's a critical part of, I think,

01:30:27.030 --> 01:30:30.070
the black experience or
racialized experience

01:30:30.070 --> 01:30:33.550
for many people, is that escape hatch.

01:30:33.550 --> 01:30:35.040
There is no escape hatch.

01:30:35.040 --> 01:30:38.933
The one other thing I would
say about that is that,

01:30:40.950 --> 01:30:43.100
is that, I think, to take them,

01:30:43.100 --> 01:30:47.740
all these sense of time and expand it

01:30:47.740 --> 01:30:52.260
over a longer span, I think, to take,

01:30:52.260 --> 01:30:54.740
I hate to utter her name,
but to take to the racial,

01:30:54.740 --> 01:30:59.740
those all example, I think
she had mixed race children

01:31:01.590 --> 01:31:06.590
who were black and she was
welcomed into the black community

01:31:07.740 --> 01:31:11.070
not as a black person, but
as a black family member.

01:31:11.070 --> 01:31:13.700
And over a couple generations,

01:31:13.700 --> 01:31:16.800
the whiteness as such would disappear.

01:31:16.800 --> 01:31:20.370
But to demand that the black time

01:31:21.320 --> 01:31:25.120
peg itself to a lifetime,

01:31:25.120 --> 01:31:27.070
I think that's just not how that works,

01:31:28.100 --> 01:31:29.200
Yeah.

01:31:29.200 --> 01:31:30.960
- Yeah, it tries to
short circuit something

01:31:30.960 --> 01:31:35.270
that is actually, it's not
just days and weeks and months,

01:31:35.270 --> 01:31:38.363
it's years and decades and generations.

01:31:39.560 --> 01:31:43.740
I'm seeing some other
questions that I think I can

01:31:48.890 --> 01:31:53.000
dispatch relatively quickly
if we have some time.

01:31:53.000 --> 01:31:58.000
So there's some questions
about whether plays or films

01:31:59.540 --> 01:32:03.460
that have to do with people
pretending to be Jewish

01:32:03.460 --> 01:32:07.430
or pretending to be a
Nazi would be included.

01:32:07.430 --> 01:32:11.630
There are some amazing race transformation

01:32:11.630 --> 01:32:14.200
in the kind of speculative fiction sense,

01:32:14.200 --> 01:32:16.670
tales about becoming Jewish.

01:32:16.670 --> 01:32:21.670
There's a kind of
horrifically anti-Semitic play

01:32:21.750 --> 01:32:25.150
that I almost considered,
but I wanted to limit this

01:32:25.150 --> 01:32:27.780
to the question of black
and white transformation

01:32:27.780 --> 01:32:28.900
because it is so prevalent

01:32:28.900 --> 01:32:30.600
and because there are so many texts.

01:32:30.600 --> 01:32:32.850
It was a way to narrow my scope somewhat.

01:32:32.850 --> 01:32:36.440
And also, I think that
the dynamics of religious

01:32:36.440 --> 01:32:38.730
quasi-ethnic transformation are different

01:32:38.730 --> 01:32:41.633
than racial transformation
in the American sense.

01:32:42.520 --> 01:32:46.040
And I'm asked if "BlacKkKlansman"
was a text I considered.

01:32:46.040 --> 01:32:49.630
I did not, because that's
simply a change of voice

01:32:49.630 --> 01:32:51.370
and it happens over the phone.

01:32:51.370 --> 01:32:54.840
The same happens in "Sorry To
Bother You" by Boots Riley,

01:32:54.840 --> 01:32:59.050
where it doesn't have the
science fictional gimmick.

01:32:59.050 --> 01:33:00.980
And the memoirs that I looked at,

01:33:00.980 --> 01:33:03.210
the non-fiction accounts that I looked at

01:33:04.120 --> 01:33:06.930
all use some version
of some kind of science

01:33:06.930 --> 01:33:09.830
to try to transform themselves.

01:33:09.830 --> 01:33:13.530
One of the text that I almost
looked at was "Tropic Thunder"

01:33:13.530 --> 01:33:16.790
because Robert Downey Jr.'s character

01:33:16.790 --> 01:33:21.100
is said to have undergone
pigment augmentation,

01:33:21.100 --> 01:33:25.830
which is similar to the
tanning pills in "Soul Man",

01:33:25.830 --> 01:33:29.120
but it's such a brief
moment of transformation

01:33:29.120 --> 01:33:31.720
and he basically appears as
black throughout the movie

01:33:31.720 --> 01:33:36.030
and so it's closer in effect
to black face or minstrelsy

01:33:36.030 --> 01:33:38.993
than it is to a race
transformation text as such.

01:33:41.330 --> 01:33:43.860
Does the assumption of race
that it is a social construct

01:33:43.860 --> 01:33:46.530
suggest that it is a caste system?

01:33:46.530 --> 01:33:49.910
I think that there are many people

01:33:49.910 --> 01:33:54.833
who are interested in
this idea, in sociology.

01:33:55.800 --> 01:33:59.907
And we had this recent
book called "Caste".

01:34:01.310 --> 01:34:02.313
What's her name?

01:34:03.890 --> 01:34:05.653
Wilkerson, I wanna say.

01:34:07.210 --> 01:34:11.590
But I read a review of that
book that put it into context.

01:34:11.590 --> 01:34:14.570
It's in the Boston Review
and it's an excellent review

01:34:14.570 --> 01:34:17.550
because it sort of pulls
apart some of the assumptions

01:34:17.550 --> 01:34:19.960
and also talks about the difficulty

01:34:19.960 --> 01:34:24.290
and perhaps the vagueness or fuzziness

01:34:24.290 --> 01:34:27.980
of applying a system of caste,

01:34:27.980 --> 01:34:32.410
which is kind of external to
the US, onto the US context.

01:34:32.410 --> 01:34:35.850
So I would hesitate to follow from that.

01:34:35.850 --> 01:34:38.320
I also don't think we need caste

01:34:38.320 --> 01:34:40.100
to understand how race works.

01:34:40.100 --> 01:34:42.470
We got a lot of analysis of how race works

01:34:42.470 --> 01:34:47.470
as a social construct in the
history of American theory

01:34:48.150 --> 01:34:50.950
and sociology.

01:34:50.950 --> 01:34:54.423
And then finally, there's a question here,

01:34:57.760 --> 01:34:58.593
well, there's two questions.

01:34:58.593 --> 01:35:03.593
There's one about teaching these texts

01:35:03.840 --> 01:35:06.890
and there's also one about

01:35:10.720 --> 01:35:15.013
journeying, I don't think I
understand this one actually.

01:35:16.227 --> 01:35:18.427
- I think the last
question is asking about,

01:35:22.730 --> 01:35:24.330
no, I have to read it again too.

01:35:28.770 --> 01:35:30.090
Go ahead.
- Sorry, sorry.

01:35:30.090 --> 01:35:31.610
Yes, so it says, would it be fair

01:35:31.610 --> 01:35:35.180
to include the going native or
white savior genre into this?

01:35:35.180 --> 01:35:40.180
And I do think that there's,
those kinds of texts

01:35:40.260 --> 01:35:45.200
do fall into the race
transformation as morality tale

01:35:46.430 --> 01:35:49.683
subsection or exhibit of my circus.

01:35:50.820 --> 01:35:55.820
But I think, again, the willful
transformation of the self

01:35:59.930 --> 01:36:04.408
as opposed to moving into
a community to save it

01:36:04.408 --> 01:36:08.690
or going native in the kind
of anthropological mode

01:36:08.690 --> 01:36:10.083
are slightly distinct.

01:36:11.050 --> 01:36:13.940
They have similar impulses
behind them, I do think,

01:36:13.940 --> 01:36:17.250
but I would, again, by
trying to sort of work

01:36:17.250 --> 01:36:20.980
with the very specific
idea of transformation,

01:36:20.980 --> 01:36:23.810
of actually physical
transformation in these texts,

01:36:23.810 --> 01:36:27.040
I'm trying to get it specifically

01:36:27.040 --> 01:36:31.010
the way that this genre
produces its own aesthetics,

01:36:31.010 --> 01:36:33.040
specifically the topsy-turviness,

01:36:33.040 --> 01:36:35.900
the satirical tone, the immediacy,

01:36:35.900 --> 01:36:38.170
the violence of the slapstick,

01:36:38.170 --> 01:36:41.600
and also the sense of reversibility

01:36:41.600 --> 01:36:45.893
or being able to go back if you can,

01:36:47.540 --> 01:36:48.720
as Tina was pointing out.

01:36:48.720 --> 01:36:52.140
So I was interested in, less in embedding

01:36:52.140 --> 01:36:53.570
than in transforming.

01:36:53.570 --> 01:36:56.240
Okay, so the final question I'll take

01:36:56.240 --> 01:36:57.750
is about teaching these texts.

01:36:57.750 --> 01:37:02.750
And so I've taught "Black
No More", "Get Out",

01:37:04.890 --> 01:37:08.027
and a bit of the Robert Gilbert Wells's

01:37:08.027 --> 01:37:12.100
"Anthropology Applied to
the White Man and Negro."

01:37:12.100 --> 01:37:17.100
I've not taught the, any of the
white authored texts before.

01:37:19.730 --> 01:37:22.380
I was considering teaching "The Menace"

01:37:22.380 --> 01:37:25.850
in juxtaposition with "Black No More",

01:37:25.850 --> 01:37:28.440
because they have so much in common,

01:37:28.440 --> 01:37:32.450
including the mechanism
of the transformation.

01:37:32.450 --> 01:37:35.363
And they were published
around the same time.

01:37:37.370 --> 01:37:40.670
It provides, again, a very
instructive juxtaposition,

01:37:40.670 --> 01:37:42.730
like the one I tried to
give you in the script

01:37:42.730 --> 01:37:46.140
for "Watermelon Man"
versus the actual direction

01:37:46.140 --> 01:37:47.723
that Melvin Van Peebles gave.

01:37:48.710 --> 01:37:50.040
But it's difficult.

01:37:50.040 --> 01:37:53.820
I mean, when I've taught
black science fiction before,

01:37:53.820 --> 01:37:58.820
I've taught Bradbury and I've taught Poe

01:37:59.080 --> 01:38:02.230
as ways of uncovering what Morrison calls

01:38:02.230 --> 01:38:05.060
the kind of Africanus
presence in their works.

01:38:05.060 --> 01:38:07.250
And I've taught a little
bit of Lovecraft as well

01:38:07.250 --> 01:38:10.610
to show what it is that black
science fiction writers,

01:38:10.610 --> 01:38:12.600
in some ways are, writing against

01:38:12.600 --> 01:38:14.060
and what they're working with

01:38:14.060 --> 01:38:16.600
in terms of a shared social set

01:38:16.600 --> 01:38:19.670
of assumptions and stereotypes.

01:38:19.670 --> 01:38:24.010
But it is difficult to teach
Ray Bradbury to undergraduates

01:38:24.010 --> 01:38:26.530
in a science fiction course, who,

01:38:26.530 --> 01:38:29.140
many of them have read
Bradbury and love Bradbury,

01:38:29.140 --> 01:38:32.080
and for me to point out that actually

01:38:32.080 --> 01:38:33.920
the story is incredibly racist.

01:38:33.920 --> 01:38:37.680
And so I think it's useful,

01:38:37.680 --> 01:38:40.480
and I don't believe in
canceling these authors.

01:38:40.480 --> 01:38:43.030
I believe that it's useful to teach them

01:38:43.030 --> 01:38:46.700
precisely with an attention
to where they fall short

01:38:46.700 --> 01:38:50.170
and to where they reflect
the ideology of their time

01:38:50.170 --> 01:38:53.500
and the racism that still exists.

01:38:53.500 --> 01:38:56.490
This is how racism kind
of perpetuates itself,

01:38:56.490 --> 01:38:59.370
is through the culture,
through the texts we read,

01:38:59.370 --> 01:39:00.940
through the things we watch.

01:39:00.940 --> 01:39:05.120
And so even when it's black
directors or black authors,

01:39:05.120 --> 01:39:07.000
everyone is kind of participating

01:39:07.000 --> 01:39:10.210
in this machine of etiology production.

01:39:10.210 --> 01:39:11.720
Understanding how that works

01:39:11.720 --> 01:39:13.330
is incredibly useful for students.

01:39:13.330 --> 01:39:16.260
And so simply not reading
it is not gonna help

01:39:16.260 --> 01:39:21.100
once they leave the classroom
and enter a post-Trump world,

01:39:21.100 --> 01:39:26.100
where discarding Haitian refugees.

01:39:27.690 --> 01:39:31.090
It's silly, I think, to pretend
that racism doesn't exist

01:39:31.090 --> 01:39:33.970
and in the text that we
